**********Extracted claims**********

*The Last Judgment* is a triptych by Hieronymus Bosch.
*The Last Judgment* was created around 1482.
The type of painting for *The Last Judgment* is an oil-on-wood triptych.
*The Last Judgment* measures 163.7 cm × 242 cm (64.4 in × 95 in).
*The Last Judgment* is located at the Academy of Fine Arts in Vienna.
*The Last Judgment* was created by the Early Netherlandish artist Hieronymus Bosch.
*The Last Judgment* was created after 1482.
The triptych is located in Vienna, Austria.
The outside of the shutters of the triptych are painted in grisaille on panel.
The inside shutters and the center panel of the triptych are painted in oil.
The left and right panels of the triptych measure 167.7 cm x 60 cm.
The center panel of the triptych measures 164 cm x 127 cm.
*The Last Judgment* (Vienna) is not to be confused with a fragmented piece of art by Bosch titled "The Last Judgment (Bosch triptych fragment)" in Munich.
*The Last Judgment* (Vienna) is not to be confused with another full painting by Bosch titled "The Last Judgment (Bosch triptych), Bruges."
The Bruges painting of *The Last Judgment* is possibly by a painter in Bosch's workshop.
The left panel of *The Last Judgment* shows the Garden of Eden.
In the upper part of the left panel, God is shown seated in Heaven.
In the upper part of the left panel, Rebel Angels are cast out of Heaven and transformed into insects.
At the foot of the left panel, God creates Eve from the rib of Adam.
In the mid-ground of the left panel, Eve is tempted by the Serpent.
Towards the center of the left panel, Adam and Eve are chased by the Angel into the dark forest.
In the central panel, Jesus judges the souls while surrounded by the Saints.
The right panel shows a hellscape.
The wicked are punished in the right panel.
The oldest mention of the painting is in a 1659 inventory of Archduke Leopold Wilhelm of Austria's collection.
The painting was mentioned as by "Hieronimo Bosz" in the 1659 inventory.
The painting was acquired by Count Lambert-Sprinzenstein in the late 18th century.
The painting later went from Count Lambert-Sprinzenstein to its current location.
The triptych was widely repainted in the 17th-18th centuries.
The triptych has lost part of its colors.
Some art historians identified this work as that acquired by Philip I of Castile in 1504.
Other art historians deny that this work is the one acquired by Philip I of Castile in 1504.
Dendrochronologic analysis proved that the painting was executed not before 1482.
A copy of the work exists.
The copy of the work is attributed to Lucas Cranach the Elder.
The copy of the work is located in the Gemäldegalerie of Berlin.
The painting's composition has similarities with the *Haywain Triptych*.
The painting's composition has similarities with *The Garden of Earthly Delights*.
The *Haywain Triptych* shows the Garden of Eden in its left panel.
The *Haywain Triptych* shows Hell at its right panel.
*The Garden of Earthly Delights* shows the Garden of Eden in its left panel.
*The Garden of Earthly Delights* shows Hell at its right panel.
The central panel depicts a Last Judgement.
The central panel has a more obscure atmosphere than the Hell panel.
The shutters are externally painted in grisaille.
The external grisaille paintings on the shutters depict two saints.
The saint on the left shutter is St. James.
St. James is depicted in pilgrimage within a wicked land with a hanged man.
The saint on the right shutter is St. Bavo.
St. Bavo is depicted donating to the poor with his hawk on his left wrist.
St. Bavo is the patron of Flanders.
An old woman with a child appears in the St. Bavo panel.
The old woman with a child in the St. Bavo panel appears in a drawing attributed to Bosch.
The drawing attributed to Bosch is located in a San Francisco private collection.
The left panel depicts the Garden of Eden of biblical history.
The Garden of Eden is depicted as a green landscape in the lower three-quarters of the left panel.
In the upper section of the left panel, Bosch portrays God sitting on his throne.
God is surrounded by a luminous halo in the painting.
Around God is a cloudy sky.
Angels are fighting rebellious angels in the cloudy sky on the left panel.
The rebellious angels are turning into devils as they fall.
In the lower part of the left panel, God creates Eve from Adam's rib.
Adam is sleeping at Eve's feet during her creation in the painting.
The Serpent tempts Eve near the tree of the knowledge of good and evil on the left panel.
Adam and Eve are expelled from the Garden by an angel.
The angel holds a sword during the expulsion.
Adam and Eve are expelled into a dark forest.
The central painting depicts a Last Judgement based on John's Book of Revelation.
Christ is depicted as a judge above in the central panel.
Christ is surrounded by the Virgin Mary, John the Evangelist, and the apostles in the central panel.
The celestial zone in the central panel is painted in a bright blue.
The celestial zone contrasts with the rest of the central panel.
The rest of the central panel is occupied by a dark brownish punishment of the Damned.
The Blessed occupy only a small portion of the central panel.
Punishments come from monstrous creatures of Hell in the central panel.
The damned are burned in the central panel.
The damned are speared in the central panel.
The damned are impaled in the central panel.
The damned are hung from butcher hooks in the central panel.
The Gluttonous are forced to eat impure food in the central panel.
The damned are subjects to cogs of bizarre machines in the central panel.
The scene in the central panel has strong similarities with the right panel in Bosch's *Garden* at the Museo del Prado.
The hell depicted on the right panel is thematically consistent with the Last Judgement.
Satan is in the center of the right panel.
Satan receives the damned souls in the right panel.
Torture scenes continue in the right panel.
The torture scenes are within a dark landscape.
The dark landscape on the right panel is dominated by flames and devilish figures.

