Title: Manjū
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manj%C5%AB
Content: Jump to content

From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Japanese confection

For other uses, see Manju "Manju (disambiguation)").

|     |     |
| --- | --- |
| ! | hide **This article has multiple issues.** Please help **improve it** or discuss these iss

**********Extracted claims**********

Manjū is a traditional Japanese confection.
Manjū is typically a small, dense bun with a sweet filling.
Manjū comes in many shapes and varieties.
Standard manjū has a skin made of flour.
Standard manjū is filled with anko.
Anko is sweet azuki bean paste.
Some manjū varieties use kudzu starch for the skin.
Some manjū varieties use buckwheat flour for the skin.
Other types of manjū filling include sweet potato.
Other types of manjū filling include chestnut jam.
Other types of manjū filling include custard.
Manjū is usually steamed or baked.
Fried manjū can be found in some modern restaurants.
Traditional manjū are usually round.
Many different shapes of manjū exist.
Some manjū shapes are proprietary to specific bakeries.
Manjū is a traditional Japanese flour-based pastry.
Manjū is not a rice-based pastry like mochi.
Japanese Buddhist monks who studied in the Song dynasty brought tea culture to Japan during the Kamakura period (1185–1333).
The custom of eating confections with tea began in Japan during the Kamakura period.
Monks introduced tenshin to Japan.
Tenshin is a light meal.
The history book *Teikun ōrai* mentions udon as tenshin.
The history book *Teikun ōrai* mentions manjū as tenshin.
The history book *Teikun ōrai* mentions yōkan as tenshin.
Monk Enni is believed to have introduced manjū production techniques in Hakata, Fukuoka Prefecture in 1241.
Monk Enni built Joten-ji in Hakata upon returning from China.
Joten-ji is a temple of the Rinzai sect.
Monk Enni gave a manjū recipe to a teahouse owner.
Monk Enni gave a hand-written signboard with the words "Omanjū Dokoro" to a teahouse owner.
The signboard "Omanjū Dokoro" is now in possession of Toraya Kurokawa in Akasaka, Tokyo.
"Omanjū Dokoro" means "Place to Eat Manjū".
A stone monument in the garden of Joten-ji temple commemorates the introduction of manjū to Japan.
The manjū brought to Japan by monks were not sweets.
The yōkan brought to Japan by monks were not sweets.
Manjū brought by monks were prepared differently than modern manjū.
Yōkan brought by monks were prepared differently than modern yōkan.
Early manjū resembled Chinese mantou.
Manjū and mantou are written with the same kanji.
Early manjū was not a confection.
Early manjū did not contain red bean paste.
Early manjū was not sweet.
The history book *Sōgo ōzōshi* describes manjū as a dish eaten with chopsticks along with soup and pickles.
Manjū changed from a light meal to a confection to suit Japanese tastes.
The *Shokunin utaai ehon* depicted sweet manjū made with sugar in the Muromachi period (1336–1573).
Sweet manjū made with sugar (depicted in *Shokunin utaai ehon*) is considered the prototype of today's manjū.
Okinawan manjū in Hawaii are made with a filling of purple sweet potato, butter, milk, sugar, and salt.
The most common manjū filling is bean paste.
Varieties of bean paste filling include koshian, tsubuan, and tsubushian.
Matcha manjū is one of the most common varieties.
Matcha manjū has a green tea flavor on the outside.
Matcha manjū is colored green.
Mizu manjū is traditionally eaten in the summertime.
Mizu manjū contains a flavored bean filling.
The exterior of mizu manjū is made with kuzu starch.
Kuzu starch gives mizu manjū dough a translucent appearance.
Kuzu starch gives mizu manjū dough a jelly-like appearance.
Horaku manjū is sweet dough.
Horaku manjū is filled with red bean paste made from adzuki beans.
Horaku manjū can be filled with white bean paste made from white kidney beans.
Horaku manjū is popular in Kyushu.
Horaku manjū is especially popular in Fukuoka, Kumamoto, Kagoshima, and Miyazaki prefectures.
A type of manjū similar to Horaku manjū is sold at a street stall in Jotenji-dori in Fukuoka.
The street stall is located near the manjū monument on the grounds of Joten-ji Temple.
Manjū can have different flavored fillings.
Orange-flavored cream is an example of a flavored filling for manjū.
Some parts of Japan have manjū unique to that region.
Momiji manjū is a maple leaf-shaped manjū.
Momiji manjū is found in Hiroshima.
Momiji manjū is found in Miyajima.
The regional variety of manjū in Saitama prefecture is called Jumangoku manjū.

