Title: Air raids on Semarang–Salatiga–Ambarawa
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_raids_on_Semarang%E2%80%93Salatiga%E2%80%93Ambarawa
Content: Jump to content

From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

1947 aerial bombing by Indonesia during Indonesian National Revolution

| Air raids on Semarang–Salatiga–Ambarawa |
| --- |
| Part of the Indonesian National Revolution |
| ! An Indonesian Air Force 

**********Extracted claims**********

The air raids on Semarang, Salatiga, and Ambarawa occurred on 29 July 1947.
The air raids were an air strike launched by the Indonesian Air Force.
The air strike targeted Dutch military objectives.
The Dutch military objectives were located in the Central Javanese town of Ambarawa and the cities of Semarang and Salatiga.
The air raids were part of the Indonesian National Revolution.
The air raids were the first military operation conducted by the Indonesian Air Force.
29 July is commemorated in Indonesia as "Air Force Service Day" (_Hari Bakti Angkatan Udara_).
Air Force Service Day commemorates the air raids and the 1947 Yogyakarta Dakota incident.
Dutch forces launched Operation Product on 21 July 1947.
Operation Product was a military offensive.
Operation Product aimed to capture economically productive areas of Indonesian Republican-held territories in Java and Sumatra.
Operation Product violated the Linggadjati Agreement.
The Linggadjati Agreement was signed in 1946.
The Royal Netherlands East Indies Army Air Force (ML-KNIL) launched Operation Pelikaan.
Operation Pelikaan aimed to attack Indonesian airfields.
Operation Pelikaan aimed to destroy aircraft belonging to the Indonesian Air Force.
Operation Pelikaan aimed to prevent Indonesian aircraft from disrupting the offensive.
24 Indonesian aircraft were destroyed between 21 and 23 July.
Ground facilities at several Indonesian airfields were disabled.
Several Indonesian Air Force cadets proposed launching air strikes against Dutch-held territory.
The air strike proposal was initially rejected by Air Force commander Air Commodore Suryadi Suryadarma and Air Vice Commodore Halim Perdanakusuma.
Air Commodore Suryadi Suryadarma and Air Vice Commodore Halim Perdanakusuma later approved the air strike proposal.
Halim assigned 05:00 on 29 July 1947 as the H-Hour for the raid.
Semarang and Salatiga were designated as targets for the raid.
Four aircraft were allocated for the mission from Maguwo airfield in the outskirts of Yogyakarta.
The allocated aircraft included two Yokosuka K5Y1 "Willow" planes, one Mitsubishi Ki-51 "Sonia" plane, and one Nakajima Ki-43 "Oscar" plane.
Two K5Y1 aircraft were assigned to target Salatiga.
Flight Cadets Sutarjo Sigit and Suharnoko Harbani were designated to fly the K5Y1s.
Sutarjo and Kaput were designated as rear gunners for the K5Y1s.
Semarang was assigned as the target for the Ki-51 aircraft.
Flight Cadet Mulyono flew the Ki-51 aircraft.
Dulrachman was the rear gunner for the Ki-51 aircraft.
The Ki-51 aircraft was escorted by a Ki-43 aircraft.
Flight Cadet Bambang Saptoaji flew the Ki-43 aircraft.
The four pilot cadets received a mission briefing from Suryadarma and Halim at 19:00 on 28 July.
The Ki-43's machine gun synchronization system could not be repaired before the mission.
The raids proceeded with three aircraft instead of four.
The defensive machine gun could not be mounted on Sutarjo Sigit's K5Y1.
The mission proceeded as planned, with aircraft departing Maguwo airfield at 05:00.
The Ki-51 flown by Mulyono was the first aircraft to take off.
K5Y1 aircraft flown by Sutarjo and Suharnoko took off after the Ki-51.
Suharnoko's aircraft separated from Sutarjo's aircraft during the flight to Salatiga.
Suharnoko dropped his bombs believing he was above Ambarawa after seeing a vast lake.
Suharnoko later rendezvoused with Sutarjo.
Sutarjo dropped one bomb over Salatiga, which hit a local hospital but failed to explode.
Another bomb dropped by Sutarjo was due to a technical malfunction.
Mulyono reached Semarang and dropped two bombs over the Port of Tanjung Emas.
The bombing at the Port of Tanjung Emas killed seven people.
All three aircraft returned safely to Maguwo airfield.
The aircraft were quickly camouflaged to prevent detection by Dutch aircraft.
The No. 120 Squadron of the Dutch East Indies Army Air Force launched offensive counter air sorties in response to the raids.
The counter air sorties targeted Indonesian airfields at Maguwo and Maospati.
Two Dutch P-40 Kittyhawks shot down a C-47 aircraft on landing approach to Maguwo during a sortie.
The shooting down of the C-47 culminated in the 1947 Yogyakarta Dakota incident.
The 1981 film *Serangan Fajar* features the air raids as a main plot point.
The 2021 Indonesian film *Cadet 1947* is a dramatized retelling of the 29 July 1947 air raids and its participants.
The air raids had an inconclusive outcome.
Indonesia sustained no casualties during the air raids.

