From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

2015 video game

2015 video game

| _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ |
| --- |
| ! Microsoft Windows cover featuring Brazil national team's Neymar |
| Developer(s) | PES Productions |
| Publisher(s) | Kon

**********Extracted claims**********

_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is a 2015 video game.
PES Productions developed _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_.
Konami published _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_.
Yoshikatsu Ogihara directed _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is part of the _Pro Evolution Soccer_ series.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ uses the Fox Engine.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is available on Microsoft Windows, PlayStation 3, PlayStation 4, Xbox 360, and Xbox One platforms.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released in North America on September 15, 2015.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released in Europe on September 17, 2015.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released in the United Kingdom on September 18, 2015.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released in Australia on September 22, 2015.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released in Japan on October 1, 2015.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is a sports genre game.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ supports single-player and multiplayer modes.
The Microsoft Windows cover of _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ features Brazil national team's Neymar.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is abbreviated as _PES 2016_.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is marketed as _Winning Eleven 2016_ in Japan.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is a football simulation game.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is the fifteenth edition of the _Pro Evolution Soccer_ series.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ marks PES Productions' 20th year of producing football games.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is marketed with the slogan "_Love The Past, Play The Future_".
The cover of _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ features Neymar Jr. wearing his Brazil national team kit.
The name of the game changed from "World Soccer: Winning Eleven" to "Pro Evolution Soccer" in Asia, excluding Japan.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2017_ succeeded _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_.
Konami extended its license for the UEFA Champions League, UEFA Europa League, and UEFA Super Cup until 2018.
Konami acquired the exclusive license for the UEFA Euro 2016 tournament.
The UEFA Euro 2016 content was not part of _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ from its initial release.
The UEFA Euro 2016 content for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released in an update on March 24, 2016.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes Ligue 1, La Liga, AFC Champions League, UEFA Champions League, UEFA Europa League, UEFA Super Cup, Copa Libertadores, Copa Sudamericana (2014 and 2015 seasons), Recopa Sudamericana, Ligue 2, Liga Adelante, and Eredivisie.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes an unlicensed Serie A with 19 licensed teams.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes an unlicensed Premier League with only Manchester United licensed.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes 3 teams from Bundesliga: Bayern Munich, Wolfsburg, and Borussia Mönchengladbach.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ does not include a stand-in for the Bundesliga league.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes an unlicensed Football League Championship.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes an unlicensed Serie B.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes an unlicensed Portuguese League with only Benfica, Sporting CP, and Porto licensed.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes an unlicensed Brazilian League.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes an unlicensed Chilean League with only the teams competing in Copa Libertadores and Copa Sudamericana licensed.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes an unlicensed Argentine League with only the teams competing in Copa Libertadores and Copa Sudamericana licensed.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ includes 4 teams from the Brazilian Second Division: Vitória, Bahia, Botafogo, and Criciúma.
The _PES 2016_ Demo was playable on August 13, 2015.
The teams available in the _PES 2016_ Demo were Bayern Munich, Juventus, AS Roma, Palmeiras, Corinthians, France National Football Team, and Brazil National Football Team.
The myClub mode in _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is known as _Winning Eleven 2016 myClub_ in Japan.
myClub is an online mode where players assemble a "dream team" from the game's database using trading cards.
The myClub mode is similar to the _FIFA_ series Ultimate Team mode.
The myClub mode is available as a stand-alone, free-to-play game.
The stand-alone myClub game was released on December 8, 2015, for PlayStation 3 and PlayStation 4.
The stand-alone myClub game was released on February 3, 2016, for Steam.
A new feature of myClub is the acquisition of "myClub Legends," which are classic players.
Examples of myClub Legends include Roberto Baggio, Roberto Carlos, Filippo Inzaghi, Luís Figo, and Oliver Kahn.
Data Pack 1 for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released on October 29, 2015.
Data Pack 1 added 8 new teams, new boot styles, new jerseys for some teams, 70 new and updated player likenesses, and updated player rosters covering transfers up to August 30.
Data Pack 1 included some changes to gameplay.
Data Pack 2 for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released on December 3, 2015.
Data Pack 2 added 51 new player likenesses, updated player rosters covering transfers up to October 19, eight new boot styles, two new ball designs, and new entrance sequences for Copa Libertadores and Sudamericana matches.
The Estadio do Maracana stadium was made available exclusively for PlayStation 4 and Xbox One in Data Pack 2.
Data Pack 3 for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released on March 24, 2016.
Data Pack 3 included official kits and player likenesses for 15 officially licensed teams participating in the UEFA Euro 2016 tournament.
The remaining 9 teams in Data Pack 3's UEFA Euro 2016 content wear generic uniforms.
Data Pack 3 included the Stade de France, which is the venue of the UEFA Euro 2016 tournament final.
Konami issued a physical and digital standalone re-release of _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ that included the Euro 2016 content.
The standalone re-release of _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was for PlayStation 4 and PlayStation 3.
The standalone re-release was released on April 21, 2016, in Europe and Japan.
The Euro 2016 mode contains all 24 teams that qualified for the 2016 tournament.
The Euro 2016 mode contains ten other national teams featured in the game that did not qualify: Bosnia and Herzegovina, Bulgaria, Denmark, Greece, Israel, Netherlands, Norway, Scotland, Serbia, and Slovenia.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is the first UEFA European Championship video game made by Konami.
Previous UEFA European Championship video games were made by Gremlin Interactive and EA Sports.
The British PlayStation 4 UEFA Euro 2016 edition cover features Gareth Bale.
A version of the cover featuring Gareth Bale was made available in Welsh.
The Welsh version of the cover is the first game cover ever to be made available in Welsh.
Data Pack 4 for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was released on June 9, 2016.
Data Pack 4 is the final update for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_.
Data Pack 4 includes official UEFA Euro 2016 kits for the national teams of Croatia, England, France, Portugal, and Turkey.
Data Pack 4 adds new official kits for Brazil, the Netherlands, CD Lugo, Santos, Flamengo, and Torino.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ received very positive reviews.
IGN scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 9.5 out of 10.
IGN stated that _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ "might well be the best football game ever made."
IGN praised _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_'s physicality and responsive, dynamic gameplay.
IGN criticized _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_'s uneven, repetitive commentary.
GameSpot scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 9 out of 10.
GameSpot referred to _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ as a "Return of the king."
GameSpot stated that "almost everything in _PES 2016_ *feels* fantastic."
GameSpot praised how _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ "wonderfully converges" the physics and AI.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ introduced a new dynamic physics engine with a new collision system.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ included improved AI, featuring dozens of smart individual decisions where players work together as a unit.
GameSpot praised _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_'s "outstanding" animations and enhanced fluidity and control.
GameSpot criticized _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_'s UI, character models, and referee lenience to some fouls.
GameSpot concluded that _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ "represents the best game in the series since the PlayStation 2 era."
Metro scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 9 out of 10.
Metro stated that "Pro Evolution Soccer is finally back to its best."
Metro stated that _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is "undoubtedly one of the greatest sports games of all-time."
Game Informer gave _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 9 out of 10.
Game Informer stated that "PES 2016 represents the sport in beautiful fashion."
Game Informer stated that "The off-the-ball movement to create give-and-gos are generated naturally, players find good spaces in the box and go for runs, and defenders jostle dribblers and cut out passes" in _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_.
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ is a more physical game.
In _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_, opposing players nip at feet, barge off the ball, and get stuck in.
Game Informer concluded that _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ "ushers in a new era for the franchise that needs to be experienced."
_Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ was nominated for Best Sports/Racing Game at The Game Awards 2015.
The GameRankings score for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ (PS4) is 84.48%.
The GameRankings score for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ (XONE) is 86.58%.
The GameRankings score for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ (PC) is 59.50%.
The Metacritic score for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ (PS4) is 87/100.
The Metacritic score for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ (XONE) is 85/100.
The Metacritic score for _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ (PC) is 76/100.
Eurogamer scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 9/10.
GamePro scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 90/100.
GameRevolution scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 5/5.
GamesMaster scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 77/100.
GamesRadar+ scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 5/5.
GamesTM scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 8/10.
PlayStation Official Magazine – Australia scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 90/100.
PlayStation Official Magazine – UK scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 9/10.
Official Xbox Magazine (UK) scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 5/5.
PC Gamer (US) scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 69/100.
Play scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 9/10.
VideoGamer.com scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 9/10.
The Telegraph scored _Pro Evolution Soccer 2016_ 5/5.

