Title: Torsion siege engine
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torsion_siege_engine
Content: Jump to content

From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Type of artillery relying on a twisting force to launch projectiles

|     |     |
| --- | --- |
| ! | This article's lead section **may be too short to adequately summarize the key points**. Please 

**********Extracted claims**********

A torsion siege engine is a type of siege engine that uses torsion to launch projectiles.
Torsion siege engines were initially developed by the ancient Macedonians.
Philip II of Macedon and Alexander the Great were specific individuals involved in the development of torsion siege engines.
Torsion siege engines were used through the Middle Ages.
The development of gunpowder artillery in the 14th century rendered torsion siege engines mostly obsolete.
Tension siege engines existed since at least the beginning of the 4th century BC.
The gastraphetes, described in Heron of Alexandria's *Belopoeica*, was a notable tension siege engine.
The gastraphetes was probably invented in Syracuse by Dionysius the Elder.
The first extant evidence of a torsion siege engine dates to approximately 338 - 326 BC.
This evidence comes from the Chalcotheca, the arsenal on the Acropolis in Athens.
The inventory of the Chalcotheca included torsion catapults and components like hair springs, catapult bases, and bolts.
Torsion-based weaponry offered much greater efficiency over tension-based weaponry.
Traditional historiography suggests the invention of two-armed torsion machines occurred circa 340 BC, during the reign of Philip II of Macedon.
Torsion machines quickly spread throughout the ancient Mediterranean.
Schools and contests emerged at the end of the 4th century BC that promoted the refinement of machine design.
Competitions involving torsion machines were often held in ancient Greece and Rome.
Students from Samos, Ceos, Cyanae, and Rhodes were highly sought after by military leaders for catapult construction.
Torsion machines were used heavily in military campaigns.
Philip V of Macedon used torsion engines during his campaigns in 219-218 BC.
Philip V's use included 150 sharp-throwers and 25 stone-throwers.
Scipio Africanus confiscated 120 large catapults, 281 small catapults, 75 ballistae, and a great number of scorpions after capturing New Carthage in 209 BC.
The Romans obtained their knowledge of artillery from the Greeks.
In ancient Roman tradition, women supposedly gave up their hair for use in catapults.
An example of women's hair being used for catapults occurred in Carthage in 148-146 BC.
Torsion artillery, especially ballistae, came into heavy usage during the First Punic War.
Torsion artillery was common by the Second Punic War.
Plautus remarked in the *Captivi* about the commonality of ballistae and catapults.
By 100 AD, Romans began to permanently mount artillery.
Previously, machines had traveled largely disassembled in carts.
Romans made the Greek ballista more portable, calling the hand-held version manuballista.
The cart-mounted type of ballista was called carroballista by the Romans.
Romans also used a one-armed torsion stone-projector named the onager.
The earliest extant evidence of the carroballista is on Trajan's Column.
Between 100 and 300 AD, every Roman legion had a battery of ten onagers and 55 cheiroballistae hauled by teams of mules.
Legionaries called ballistarii had the exclusive purpose of producing, moving, and maintaining catapults.
In later antiquity, the onager began to replace more complicated two-armed devices.
Greeks and Romans were able to readily produce the many pieces needed to build a ballista due to advanced methods of military supply and armament.
In the later 4th and 5th centuries, as administrative structures changed, simpler devices became preferable.
The technical skills needed to produce more complex machines were no longer as common in the later 4th and 5th centuries.
Vegetius, Ammianus Marcellinus, and the anonymous *De rebus bellicis* are the first and most descriptive sources on torsion machines, all writing in the 4th century AD.
Procopius provides his description of torsion devices in the 6th century.
All these sources use the term ballistae and provide descriptions similar to those of their predecessors.
Torsion siege engines, such as the ballista or onager, were only used in the West until the 6th-8th centuries.
Torsion siege engines were replaced by the traction trebuchet, also known as the mangonel, in the West.
The myth of the torsion mangonel began in the 18th century when Francis Grose claimed the onager was the dominant medieval artillery until the arrival of gunpowder.
Guillaume Henri Dufour argued in the mid-19th century that onagers went out of use in medieval times and were directly replaced by the counterweight trebuchet.
Dufour and Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte argued that torsion machines were abandoned because the requisite supplies for building the sinew skein and metal support pieces were too difficult to obtain compared to materials for tension and counterweight machines.
Sir Ralph Payne-Gallwey concurred in the early 20th century that torsion catapults were not used in medieval times due to their greater complexity.
General Köhler disagreed, arguing that torsion machines were used throughout the Middle Ages.
In 1910, Rudolph Schneider pointed out that medieval Latin texts completely lack descriptions of the torsion mechanism.
Schneider proposed that all medieval terms for artillery actually referred to the trebuchet.
Schneider believed that the knowledge to build torsion engines had been lost since classical times.
In 1941, Kalervo Huuri argued that the onager remained in use in the Mediterranean region until the 7th century.
Huuri claimed that the employment of the onager became obscured in terminology as the traction trebuchet came into use.
By the 9th century, there is virtually no evidence, textual or artistic, of torsion engines used in warfare in Western Europe, with the exception of bolt throwers.
The first Western European reference to a mangana (mangonel) appeared by the 9th century.
The last historical texts specifying a torsion engine, aside from bolt throwers like the springald, date no later than the 6th century.
Illustrations of an onager do not reappear until the 15th century.
With the exception of bolt throwers such as the springald (13th-14th centuries) or the ziyar in the Muslim world, torsion machines had largely disappeared by the 6th century.
Torsion machines were replaced by the traction trebuchet.
Classical texts describing torsion machines were circulated in medieval times.
Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou, had a copy of Vegetius at the siege of Montreuil-Bellay in 1147.
The weapon used at the siege of Montreuil-Bellay in 1147 was a traction trebuchet, not a torsion catapult.
There is no evidence for the continuation of the onager in Byzantium beyond the end of the 6th century.
The absence of the onager in 'barbarian' successor kingdoms is indicated by the lack of reference and decline in expertise.
When the mangonel appeared in Europe from the east, it was a traction-propelled stone thrower.
Torsion power went out of use for approximately seven centuries.
Torsion power returned in the guise of the bolt-throwing springald.
The springald was deployed to defend walls against human assailants, not as an offensive, wall-breaking siege engine.
Mangonel was used as a general medieval term for stone-throwing artillery.
The term "mangonel" probably meant a traction trebuchet from the 6th to 12th centuries.
The mangonel was used between the disappearance of the onager and the arrival of the counterweight trebuchet.
Gustav Köhler contended at the end of the 19th century that the petrary was a traction trebuchet, invented by Muslims.
Köhler believed the mangonel was a torsion catapult.
A Welsh text from 1866 translated "mangonel" as a torsion weapon like the ballista.
"Ballista" was used in medieval times as a general term for stone-throwing machines.
Otto of Freising referred to the mangonel as a type of ballista, indicating they both threw stones.
During the siege of Paris in 885-886, seven Danes were impaled at once with a bolt from a funda.
Traction trebuchets were capable of a much higher rate of fire than torsion engines.
Traction trebuchets were far simpler to construct, use, and maintain than torsion engines.
"Traction trebuchet" is a modern term not found in contemporary sources.
Some consider "mangonel" not a specific type of siege weapon but a general term for any pre-cannon stone-throwing artillery.
Onagers have been called "onager mangonels."
Traction trebuchets have been called "beam-sling mangonel machines."
In early designs, torsion machines were made with square wooden frames with holes drilled in the top and bottom.
A skein was threaded through the holes and wrapped around wooden levers to adjust tension.
The problem with early designs was friction caused by contact between the wood of the lever and the wood of the frame when increasing tension.
The problem was solved by adding metal washers in the holes of the frames.
Washers were fastened with tenons or rims.
Washers enabled greater control over the machine's tension and maximization of power without sacrificing frame integrity.
Standard design modifications included combining two separate spring frames into a single unit to increase durability and stability.
A padded heel block was added to stop the recoil of the machine.
Formulae were developed to determine the appropriate engine size.
A ratcheting trigger mechanism was developed to make firing quicker.
Marsden suggests initial developments occurred fairly rapidly, potentially over a few decades.
Heron of Alexandria laid out the general course of torsion machine development.
Only a few specific designs of torsion catapults are known from ancient and medieval history.
Materials used were generally wood or metal.
The skein comprising the spring was specifically cited as made of animal sinew and hair, either women's or horse.
Heron and Vegetius considered sinew to be better for skeins.
Vitruvius cited women's hair as preferable for skeins.
The preferred type of sinews came from the feet of deer and the necks of oxen.
Animal sinews and hair ropes were treated with olive oil and animal grease/fat to preserve elasticity.
The energy-storing capacity of sinew is much greater than that of a wooden beam or bow.
Wood's performance in tension devices is severely affected by temperatures above 77 °F (25 °C).
Two general formulas were used to determine the size of the machine and the projectile it throws.
The first formula, for a sharp-thrower, is d = x / 9, where d is the diameter of the hole in the frame and x is the length of the bolt.
The second formula, for a stone thrower, is d=(1.1)100m^(1/3), where d is the diameter of the hole and m is the weight of the stone.
The purpose of these formulas was to maximize the potential energy of the skein.
If the skein was too long, the machine could not be used at its full capacity.
If the skein was too short, it produced high internal friction, reducing durability.
Accurately determining the diameter of the frame's holes prevented damage to the sinews and fibers of the skein.
Once initial measurements were made, corollary formulae could determine the dimensions of the rest of the machines.
d is measured in dactyls, and 1 dactyl equals 1.93 cm (0.76 in).
m is measured in minas, and 1 mina equals 437 g (15.4 oz).
1 talent equals 60 mina or 26 kg (57 lb).
No definitive results from documentation or experiment accurately verify claims about the range and damaging capabilities of torsion machines.
Constructing full-scale devices using period techniques and supplies would be necessary to test the legitimacy of design specifications and power effectiveness.
Kelly DeVries and Serafina Cuomo claim torsion engines needed to be about 150 m (490 ft) or closer to their target to be effective.
Athenaeus Mechanicus cited a three-span catapult that could propel a shot 700 yd (640 m).
Josephus cited an engine that could hurl a stone ball 400 yd (370 m) or more.
Marsden claimed most engines were probably effective up to the distance cited by Josephus, with more powerful machines going farther.
Exceptionally large projectiles have been mentioned in accounts.
Most Hellenistic projectiles found in the Near East weigh less than 15 kg (33 lb).
Most projectiles dating to the Roman period weigh less than 5 kg (11 lb).
Devices powered primarily by animal tissue had the potential to deteriorate rapidly.
Such devices could be severely affected by changing weather.
The rough surface of wooden frames could easily damage the sinew of the skein.
The force of the tension provided by the skein could potentially damage the wooden frame.
Washers placed inside the holes of the frame prevented damage to the skein and increased structural integrity.
Washers allowed engineers to precisely adjust tension levels using evenly spaced holes on their outer rim.
The skein could be made out of human or animal hair.
The skein was most commonly made out of animal sinew.
Heron specifically cited animal sinew for skeins.
The life of sinew has been estimated to be about eight to ten years.
Sinew skeins were expensive to maintain.
Torsion machines were used to provide covering fire while the attacking army assaulted a fortification.
Torsion machines were used to provide covering fire while the attacking army filled in a ditch.
Torsion machines were used to provide covering fire while other siege engines were brought up to walls.
Jim Bradbury claimed torsion engines were only useful against personnel.
Medieval torsion devices were not powerful enough to batter down walls.
Archaeological evidence for catapults, especially torsion devices, is rare.
Stones from stone-throwers could survive.
Organic sinews and wooden frames quickly deteriorate if left unattended.
Usual remains include washers, and other metal supporting pieces like counterplates and trigger mechanisms.
The first major evidence of ancient or medieval catapults was found in 1912 in Ampurias.
New catapult finds were discovered in 1968-1969 at Gornea and Orşova.
More frequent discoveries of catapult remains occurred after 1972, starting with Hatra.
The sites listed contained stone projectiles ranging in size from 10-90 minas (approximately 4.5–39 kg or 9.9–86.0 lb).
5,600 stone balls were found in Carthage (Tunisia).
961 stone balls were found in Pergamum (Turkey).
353 stone balls were found in Rhodes (Greece).
Over 200 stone balls were found in Tel Dor (Israel).
Approximately 200 stone balls were found in Salamis (Cyprus).
Archaeological remains of catapults have been found in various locations, including Ampurias (Spain), Auerberg (Germany), Azaila (Spain), Bath (United Kingdom), Caminreal (Spain), Cremona (Italy), Elginhaugh (United Kingdom), Ephyra (Greece), Gornea (Romania), Hatra (Iraq), Lyon (France), Mahdia (Tunisia), Orşova (Romania), Pergamon (Turkey), Pityous (Georgia), Sala, Sounion (Greece), Tanais (Ukraine), Volubilis (Morocco), and Xanten (Germany).
Diodorus of Sicily stated that the catapult was invented in Syracuse in 399 BC.
Josephus stated that a single projectile from these weapons ran through a row of men.
Josephus stated that the momentum of a stone hurled by the engine carried away battlements and knocked off corners of towers.
Josephus stated that no body of men is so strong that it cannot be laid low by the impact of these huge stones.
Josephus stated that a man near Josephus (the commander) on the rampart had his head knocked off by a stone, with his skull flung more than 600 m (2,000 ft).
Josephus stated that a pregnant woman struck in the belly had her unborn child carried away 100 m (330 ft).
Procopius described a Goth being hit by a missile from an engine, passing through his corselet and body, and pinning him to a tree.
There is controversy over the terminology used to describe siege engines, including torsion machines.
Manuscripts are vague in their descriptions and inconsistent in their usage of terms for siege engines.
In instances where torsion engines are identifiable, the specific type of machine is never certain.
Some scholars argue that the abundance of terms indicates widespread use of torsion devices during the Middle Ages.
Other scholars argue that the confusion about machine terminology proves that few ancient texts that survived in the Latin West did not provide adequate information for the continuation of ancient torsion machines.

**********Claims for hard query**********

Such devices could be severely affected by changing weather.
d is measured in dactyls, and 1 dactyl equals 1.93 cm (0.76 in).
The problem was solved by adding metal washers in the holes of the frames.

