Title: Norway during the Great Northern War
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway_during_the_Great_Northern_War
Content: Jump to content

From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Role of Norway in the Great Northern War

!,_m%C3%A5lning_av_Gustaf_Cederstr%C3%B6m_(1845-1933).jpg) _Bringing Home the Body of King Karl XII of Sweden_ (Gustaf Cederström. 1884)

The Great Northern 

**********Extracted claims**********

The Great Northern War was fought between a coalition of Denmark–Norway, Russia, and Saxony-Poland on one side and Sweden on the other side.
Prussia and Hanover joined the coalition against Sweden from 1715.
The Great Northern War lasted from 1700 to 1721.
The war began with a coordinated attack on Sweden by the coalition in 1700.
The war concluded in 1721 with the Treaty of Nystad and the Stockholm Treaties.
Russia supplanted Sweden as the dominant power on the shores of the Baltic Sea as a result of the war.
Russia became a major player in European politics after the war.
Sweden established an empire between 1561 and 1658 through a series of wars in the Baltic.
Sweden occupied the Danish provinces of Skåne, Blekinge, and Halland during its empire-building period.
Sweden occupied the Norwegian provinces of Jämtland, Härjedalen, Trøndelag, and Bohuslän during its empire-building period.
Swedish interference in Holstein was a major point of contention for Denmark.
Russia allied with Denmark–Norway and the Duke of Saxony in the late 1690s.
The Duke of Saxony expected to strengthen his domestic position by reconquering territory lost by his kingdom, Poland.
Charles XII ascended Sweden's throne in 1697.
The three powers attacked Sweden in 1700.
The early part of the war consisted of a continual string of Swedish victories under Charles XII.
Denmark was defeated in the summer of 1700 and withdrew from the war until 1709.
Russia suffered a defeat in the Battle of Narva in November 1700.
Russia regained the offensive after the Battle of Narva.
The city of St. Petersburg was founded between 1703 and 1707.
Charles XII defeated Augustus the Strong between 1706 and 1707.
Charles XII temporarily deposed Augustus the Strong from the Polish throne.
Frederick IV of Denmark and Norway distrusted the nobility and gentry.
Frederick IV surrounded himself with ministers and advisers of humble origin.
Ulrich Christian Gyldenløve, the king's half-brother, was General-Admiral and Commander in Chief of the Navy at age 24.
Frederick IV toured Norway in 1704 to assess the condition of his army and his second kingdom.
Frederick IV held audiences with various people during his tour of Norway.
Charles XII was defeated by Peter in the Battle of Poltava in June 1709.
Charles XII's army was virtually annihilated at the Battle of Poltava.
The Battle of Poltava occurred in modern-day Ukraine.
Charles XII fled to the Ottoman Empire and spent five years in exile there after the Battle of Poltava.
Denmark invaded Skåne in November 1709.
Count Magnus Stenbock was charged with the defense of Sweden.
Count Magnus Stenbock defeated Denmark at the Battle of Helsingborg in 1710.
Russia captured Livonia and Estonia in the east.
Sweden achieved another victory over the Danes at Gadebusch in December 1712.
Stenbock’s army was forced to surrender the fortress of Tönning in May 1713.
Norwegian forces were ordered to mobilise in September 1709.
By the end of October 1709, 6,000 Norwegian men were assembled on the Swedish border at Svinesund.
By the end of October 1709, 1,500 Norwegian men were congregated near the border at Kongsvinger Fortress.
Baron Waldemar Løvendal returned to Norway as Governor in August 1710.
Baron Løvendal worked to build the civil and military leadership in Norway.
Baron Løvendal instituted reforms in Norway by 1712 that created a civil service.
Baron Løvendal documented state activities to an unprecedented degree in Norway.
Baron Løvendal was a strong military leader.
Baron Løvendal raised and equipped a Norwegian Army to invade and recapture Bohuslän.
General-Lieutenant Caspar Herman Hausmann led the Norwegian Army intended for Bohuslän.
Frederick IV committed to providing a strong fleet under Vice Admiral Sehested in June 1711.
The Norwegian army marched into Bohuslän in August 1711.
Vice Admiral Sehested’s fleet had not appeared offshore by late summer 1711.
Frederick IV ordered Vice Admiral Sehested’s fleet to return to Baltic waters.
The Norwegian Army was forced to return to Norway due to lack of naval support.
The plague struck Denmark in the autumn of 1711.
Copenhagen alone lost 70,000 occupants due to the plague in 1711.
Frederick IV directed Løvendal in 1712 not to use the Norwegian Army in offensive actions.
The Norwegian Army was reserved for defense and for supplementing Danish troops elsewhere in 1712.
General Hausmann was named head of all Norway’s land and sea defenses in 1712.
Taxations and forced mobilization of Norwegian farm boys led to uprisings and riots in some parts of Norway.
A farmer's tax rebellion occurred in Hallingdal in 1713.
A soldier's strike occurred in Telemark in 1720.
Two leading farmers in Hallingdal spoke against Danish authorities in 1713, demanding an end to war taxation.
Tax collectors encountered closed doors throughout the Hallingdal region in 1713.
The two Hallingdal leaders were tried and sentenced to labor in Akershus fortress.
Elling Villand, one of the Hallingdal leaders, was freed less than half a year later and returned to his farm.
Olav Olavsson Hovdejord, a local sheriff and farmer in Telemark, opposed a German captain in 1720.
Olav Olavsson Hovdejord demanded an end to the forced mobilization of farmer's sons in 1720.
Norwegians were tired of the war by 1720.
The case involving Olav Hovdejord was decided in favor of the farmer.
The German captain was recalled to Denmark for his coarse behavior.
The German captain had cut off Olav Hovdejord's right hand for interfering.
Chancellor Count Arvid Horn held Sweden in the war until Charles XII returned.
Charles XII returned from the Ottoman Empire, arriving in Swedish-held Stralsund in November 1714.
Charles XII was at war with most of Northern Europe by November 1714.
Stralsund was considered doomed upon Charles XII's return.
Charles XII remained in Stralsund until December 1715.
Charles XII escaped Stralsund only days before it fell.
Many considered Charles XII mad because he would not consider peace.
All of Sweden’s Baltic and German possessions were lost by December 1715.
Charles XII began gathering materials and men for a campaign against Copenhagen upon his return to Sweden.
Charles XII planned to attack Copenhagen across the frozen Øresund from Skåne.
The ice went out before Charles XII could launch his attack on Copenhagen.
Charles XII redirected his focus to invade Norway through Bohuslän.
General Caspar Herman Hausmann warned Frederick IV of Charles XII's intent to invade Norway.
Frederick IV did not believe General Hausmann and dismissed him.
The Norwegian Army had been weakened in early 1716 by the withdrawal of 5000 of its best troops to support Denmark's defense.
Norwegian Commander-in-Chief Barthold Heinrich von Lützow was not famous as a visionary leader.
All remaining troops in Østerdal and Gudbrandsdal were ordered to the border at Halden and Fredrikstad when rumors of Charles XII's invasion reached Christiania.
The Norwegians anticipated attack from Sweden at Kongsvinger, Basmo, or Halden.
Charles XII struck at Basmo, crossing the border and establishing his headquarters at the Høland parsonage on March 8, 1716.
The Norwegian commander of the district attacked Charles XII’s superior forces with 200 dragoons.
The Norwegian commander suffered serious losses and was captured.
General Lützow withdrew his forward troops and established a defensive position at Christiania after the defeat at Basmo.
Swedish forces advanced and, leaving a strengthened garrison in Akershus fortress, Lützow withdrew to Bragernes in Drammen on March 19.
The Norwegian scorched earth policy and guerrilla raids interdicted Swedish supply chains.
Norwegian fortresses behind Swedish lines threatened Charles XII's retreat.
Charles XII took the city of Christiania.
Charles XII was unable to take Akershus Fortress without heavy siege artillery.
Charles XII retreated to the Norwegian fortresses in south-eastern Norway.
Charles XII aimed to capture Fredriksten.
The capture of Fredriksten would remove a threat at Charles XII's back.
The captured fortresses were intended to serve as a base for Charles XII's offensive later that year.
The capture of the harbors at the mouth of the Glomma river would allow Charles XII to land provisions for a siege of Akershus.
Charles XII's troops attempted to take Fredriksten by storm on July 4.
Charles XII's troops took the town of Fredriksten after fierce fighting.
The citizens of Fredriksten set fire to their homes.
Charles XII was unable to take Fredriksten fortress and was forced to retreat.
Charles XII awaited the arrival of heavy guns after the Battle of Dynekilen.
Commodore Johan Vibe of the Dano-Norwegian fleet was charged with interdicting Swedish supplies off Bohuslän.
Peter Wessel had distinguished himself in numerous engagements off the coast of Swedish Pomerania.
Peter Wessel was ennobled by Frederick IV under the name Tordenskiold in early 1716.
Captain Peter Wessel Tordenskiold led a cutting-out raid at Dynekil Fjord in Bohuslän.
The raid at Dynekil Fjord captured or destroyed the entire Swedish transport fleet.
The raid at Dynekil Fjord captured or destroyed Swedish supplies.
Charles XII retreated hastily across the Svinesund after the raid, running low on supplies.
Charles XII burned his bridges behind him during his retreat.
No Swedish soldier remained in Norway by July 12, 1716.
Frederick IV promoted Tordenskiold to Commodore for his feat at Dynekil.
Commodore Tordenskjold was given charge of a North Sea Squadron in October 1716.
Commodore Tordenskjold was placed at the head of Norway’s Sea Force over the protests of Baron Wedel.
Charles XII still planned a Norwegian invasion even after his 1716 defeat.
1717 became a year of rebuilding for both sides in the war.
Action in 1717 was limited to unsuccessful attacks by Commodore Tordenskjold's squadron on Gothenburg and Strömstad.
Commodore Tordenskjold was relieved of Command of the North Sea fleet as a result of the failures in 1717.
Commodore Tordenskjold was reassigned to the Baltic Fleet, in command of the 64-gun ship of the line, _Ebenezer_.
The North Sea fleet under Rear Admiral Andreas Rosenpalm pursued an unaggressive patrol strategy along the Bohuslän coast.
Steady streams of supplies were carried through the Bohuslän skerries to the Norwegian border throughout the summer of 1718.
Armaments emphasized heavy guns, ammunition, and supplies necessary to take the border fortresses at Fredriksten in Fredrikshald.
Charles XII attacked Norway again in the Autumn of 1718.
Charles XII was killed while inspecting the front trenches of the siege-works against Fredriksten.
Charles XII's death precipitated an immediate retreat of his forces back to Sweden.
Charles XII's death effectively terminated the Norwegian campaigns.
The war concluded in 1721.
Sweden lost almost all of her overseas holdings gained in the 17th century.
Sweden was no longer a major power after the war.
Russia took the Baltic territories.
Russia became the major power in the east after the war.
The balance of power between Sweden and Denmark-Norway was restored after the war.

**********Claims for hard query**********

The Battle of Poltava occurred in modern-day Ukraine.
Frederick IV surrounded himself with ministers and advisers of humble origin.
The city of St. Petersburg was founded between 1703 and 1707.

**********Formulated hard query**********

The Battle of Poltava occurred in somewhere.
Someone surrounded himself with ministers and advisers of humble origin.
Something was founded between 1703 and 1707.

**********Claims for medium query**********

Frederick IV of Denmark and Norway distrusted the nobility and gentry.
Sweden established an empire between 1561 and 1658 through a series of wars in the Baltic.
The raid at Dynekil Fjord captured or destroyed the entire Swedish transport fleet.

**********Formulated medium query**********

Someone distrusted the nobility and gentry.
Sweden established an empire at a certain time through a series of wars in the Baltic.
The raid at Dynekil Fjord captured or destroyed something.

