From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Mexican-Israeli physicist (1947–2015)

| Jacob Bekenstein |
| --- |
| יעקב בקנשטיין |
| !.JPG) Bekenstein in 2009 |
| Born | (1947-05-01)May 1, 1947 Mexico City, Mexico |
| Died | August 16, 2015(201

**********Extracted claims**********

Jacob Bekenstein was a Mexican-born American-Israeli theoretical physicist.
Jacob Bekenstein made fundamental contributions to the foundation of black hole thermodynamics.
Jacob Bekenstein made fundamental contributions to other aspects of the connections between information and gravitation.
Jacob Bekenstein was born on May 1, 1947, in Mexico City, Mexico.
Jacob Bekenstein died on August 16, 2015, in Helsinki, Finland.
Jacob Bekenstein received an undergraduate degree from the Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn.
Jacob Bekenstein received a Master of Science degree from the Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn.
Jacob Bekenstein received a Doctor of Philosophy degree from Princeton University.
Jacob Bekenstein's doctoral advisor was John Archibald Wheeler.
Jacob Bekenstein was known for the Bekenstein bound.
Jacob Bekenstein was known for the Bekenstein–Hawking formula.
Jacob Bekenstein was known for black hole thermodynamics.
Jacob Bekenstein was known for the no-hair theorem.
Jacob Bekenstein was known for AQUAL.
Jacob Bekenstein was known for Tensor–vector–scalar gravity.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Rothschild Prize in Physics in 1988.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Israel Prize in 2005.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Racah Lecture in 2006.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Wolf Prize in Physics in 2012.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Einstein Prize (APS) in 2015.
Jacob Bekenstein's field was theoretical physics.
Jacob Bekenstein's institutions included the Hebrew University of Jerusalem and Ben-Gurion University of the Negev.
Jacob Bekenstein was born to Joseph and Esther (née Vladaslavotsky).
Joseph and Esther Bekenstein were Polish Jews who immigrated to Mexico.
Jacob Bekenstein moved to the United States during his early life.
Jacob Bekenstein gained U.S. citizenship in 1968.
Jacob Bekenstein was a citizen of Israel.
The Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn is now known as the New York University Tandon School of Engineering.
Jacob Bekenstein obtained his undergraduate and Master of Science degrees in 1969.
Jacob Bekenstein received his Doctor of Philosophy degree in 1972.
Jacob Bekenstein had three children with his wife, Bilha.
Jacob Bekenstein's children, Yehonadav, Uriya, and Rivka Bekenstein, all became scientists.
Jacob Bekenstein was known as a religious man and a believer.
By 1972, Jacob Bekenstein had published three influential papers about the black hole stellar phenomenon.
Jacob Bekenstein postulated the no-hair theorem.
Jacob Bekenstein presented a theory on black hole thermodynamics.
Jacob Bekenstein continued his exploration of black holes in later years.
Jacob Bekenstein published papers on black hole entropy and quantum mass.
Jacob Bekenstein was a postdoctoral fellow at the University of Texas at Austin from 1972 to 1974.
Jacob Bekenstein immigrated to Israel to lecture and teach at Ben-Gurion University in Beersheba.
Jacob Bekenstein became a full professor in 1978.
Jacob Bekenstein became head of the astrophysics department in 1983.
Jacob Bekenstein became a professor at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem in 1990.
Jacob Bekenstein was appointed head of the theoretical physics department at Hebrew University three years after becoming a professor there.
Jacob Bekenstein was elected to the Israel Academy of Sciences and Humanities in 1997.
Jacob Bekenstein was a visiting scholar at the Institute for Advanced Study in 2009 and 2010.
Jacob Bekenstein continued to serve as Polak professor of theoretical physics at the Hebrew University until his death.
Jacob Bekenstein died unexpectedly.
Jacob Bekenstein received the American Physical Society's Einstein Prize for his ground-breaking work on black hole entropy.
Jacob Bekenstein's work launched the field of black hole thermodynamics.
Jacob Bekenstein's work transformed the long effort to unify quantum mechanics and gravitation.
In 1972, Jacob Bekenstein was the first to suggest that black holes should have a well-defined entropy.
Jacob Bekenstein wrote that a black hole's entropy was proportional to the area of its event horizon.
Jacob Bekenstein formulated the generalized second law of thermodynamics for systems including black holes.
Jacob Bekenstein's contributions were affirmed by Stephen Hawking's proposal of Hawking radiation.
Stephen Hawking initially opposed Jacob Bekenstein's idea that a black hole could not radiate energy.
In 1974, Stephen Hawking performed a lengthy calculation that convinced him particles can be emitted from black holes.
The emission of particles from black holes is known as Hawking radiation.
Jacob Bekenstein's doctoral adviser, John Archibald Wheeler, worked with him to develop the no-hair theorem.
The no-hair theorem is a reference to John Wheeler's saying that "black holes have no hair."
Jacob Bekenstein's suggestion regarding the no-hair theorem was proven unstable.
Jacob Bekenstein's suggestion was influential in the development of the field.
Jacob Bekenstein demonstrated the Bekenstein bound based on his black-hole thermodynamics work.
The Bekenstein bound states there is a maximum to the amount of information that can potentially be stored in a given finite region of space which has a finite amount of energy.
The Bekenstein bound is similar to the holographic principle.
In 1982, Jacob Bekenstein developed a rigorous framework to generalize the laws of electromagnetism to handle inconstant physical constants.
Jacob Bekenstein's framework replaces the fine-structure constant by a scalar field.
Jacob Bekenstein's framework for changing constants did not incorporate gravity.
In 2004, Jacob Bekenstein boosted Mordehai Milgrom's theory of Modified Newtonian Dynamics (MOND).
Jacob Bekenstein developed a relativistic version of MOND.
Jacob Bekenstein's relativistic version of MOND is known as TeVeS for Tensor/Vector/Scalar.
TeVeS introduces three different fields in spacetime to replace the one gravitational field.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Ernst David Bergmann Prize for Science (Israel) in 1977.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Landau Prize for Research in Physics (Israel) in 1981.
Jacob Bekenstein received the First prize essay for the Gravity Research Foundation (United States) in 1981.
Jacob Bekenstein was elected to the World Jewish Academy of Sciences in 2003.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Second prize essay for the Gravity Research Foundation in 2001.
Jacob Bekenstein received the Weizmann Prize in the Exact Sciences (Tel Aviv, Israel) in 2011.
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Information in the Holographic Universe".
Jacob Bekenstein is a co-author of "Quantum Limitations on the Storage and Transmission of Information".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Entropy content and information flow in systems with limited energy".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Communication and energy".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Entropy bounds and the second law for black holes".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Specific entropy and the sign of the energy".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Black holes and everyday physics".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Universal upper bound to entropy-to-energy ratio for bounded systems".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Energy cost of information transfer".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Black-hole thermodynamics".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Statistical black hole thermodynamics".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Generalized second law of thermodynamics in black hole physics".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Black holes and entropy".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Black holes and the second law".
Jacob Bekenstein is the author of "Nonexistence of baryon number of static black holes".

**********Claims for hard query**********

TeVeS introduces three different fields in spacetime to replace the one gravitational field.
The emission of particles from black holes is known as Hawking radiation.
The Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn is now known as the New York University Tandon School of Engineering.

