Title: Tennessee Volunteers football
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennessee_Volunteers_football
Content: Jump to content

From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Football team of the University of Tennessee

| Tennessee Volunteers football |
| --- |
| |     |     |
| --- | --- |
| ! | 2025 Tennessee Volunteers football team | |
| ! |
| First season | 1891; 13

**********Extracted claims**********

The Tennessee Volunteers football program represents the University of Tennessee.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program is also known as "Vols," "UT," and "Big Orange."
The Tennessee Volunteers football program has played football for 132 seasons, starting in 1891.
The combined record of the Tennessee Volunteers football program is 870–415–53 (.670).
The Tennessee Volunteers football program's combined record ranks them fourteenth on the all-time win list for NCAA football programs.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program's all-time ranking in bowl appearances is fifth (55).
The Tennessee Volunteers football program's all-time ranking in bowl victories is eighth (30).
The Tennessee Volunteers football program has won 16 conference championships.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program claims six national titles.
Two of the Tennessee Volunteers football program's claimed national titles (1951, 1998) were awarded by major wire-service polls (AP Poll and/or Coaches' Poll).
The Tennessee Volunteers football program plays at Neyland Stadium.
Neyland Stadium is located on the University of Tennessee campus in Knoxville.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program has won 485 games at Neyland Stadium.
Neyland Stadium has the highest home-field total in college football history for any school in the nation at its current home venue, with 494 on-field wins.
Neyland Stadium has a capacity of 101,915 seats.
Neyland Stadium is the nation's sixth largest stadium by capacity.
Neyland Stadium is the third largest stadium in the Southeastern Conference by capacity.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program was an Independent team from 1891–1895.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program was affiliated with the Southern Intercollegiate Athletic Association (SIAA) from 1896–1920.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program was affiliated with the Southern Conference (SoCon) from 1921–1932.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program has been affiliated with the Southeastern Conference (SEC) from 1933 to the present.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program's main rivalries include the Alabama Crimson Tide and the Vanderbilt Commodores.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program's longest and most played rivalry is with the Kentucky Wildcats.
Since the formation of the SEC Eastern Division in 1992, the Tennessee Volunteers football program has had emerging rivalries with the Florida Gators, Georgia Bulldogs, and South Carolina Gamecocks.
None of the current Tennessee Volunteers football program's rivalry games have trophies.
The Kentucky–Tennessee rivalry used to involve a trophy called the Beer Barrel from 1925 until 1999.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program used to have important rivalries with the Georgia Tech Yellow Jackets, Auburn Tigers, and Ole Miss Rebels.
The Georgia Tech Yellow Jackets left the SEC.
Realignment forced the Tennessee Volunteers football program to drop Auburn and Ole Miss from their annual schedule.
Tennessee has been selected as national champions six times by NCAA-designated major selectors.
The Associated Press (AP) has selected Tennessee as national champions twice, in 1951 and 1998.
The No. 1 Tennessee Volunteers football program lost in the Sugar Bowl following the 1951 season after being named AP and UPI national champions.
Before 1965 for the AP Poll and 1974 for the UPI poll, national champions were named before the bowl season.
The 1938 and 1950 Tennessee Volunteers football program championships, while not AP titles, were recognized by a majority and a plurality of overall selectors/polls, respectively.
Tennessee was awarded a national championship by various notable organizations in 1956, but the school does not claim this title.
Tennessee has won 13 SEC championships.
Tennessee has made five appearances in the SEC Championship Game as winners of the Southeastern Conference's Eastern Division.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program's most recent SEC Championship Game appearance was in 2007.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program has a 2–3 record in SEC Championship Games.
Tennessee has had 24 head coaches since the 1891 season.
Robert Neyland is the leader in seasons coached for the Tennessee Volunteers football program.
Robert Neyland is the leader in games won for the Tennessee Volunteers football program, with 173 victories in 21 seasons.
John Barnhill has the highest winning percentage for Tennessee coaches who coached more than one game, with .846.
James DePree has the lowest winning percentage for Tennessee coaches who coached more than one game, with .306.
Five former Tennessee head coaches (Neyland, Wyatt, Dickey, Majors, and Fulmer) have been inducted into the College Football Hall of Fame.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program holds an all-time bowl game record of 30–25 through the 2023 season, reflecting the removal of a vacated win from 2019.
The Tennessee Volunteers football program's all-time on-field bowl record is 31–25 (.554).
The Volunteers originally wore black uniforms from 1911 to 1920.
Orange jerseys with black wool numbers were first worn by the Volunteers on September 23, 1922.
The first game where orange jerseys with black wool numbers were worn was a 50–0 win against Emory & Henry.
In 1935, the Volunteers' jerseys were white with orange stripes on the sleeves.
In 1936, the Volunteers' jerseys changed to orange with white numbers.
White numbers first appeared on the front of the Volunteers' jerseys in 1936.
In 2009, the Volunteers wore black jerseys with orange pants on Halloween night against the South Carolina Gamecocks.
On October 5, 2013, the team debuted its "Smokey Gray" uniforms.
The first game where "Smokey Gray" uniforms were debuted was an overtime loss to the Georgia Bulldogs at Neyland Stadium.
In 2024, the team continues with "Smokey Gray" as the main uniform color.
For the first time in 2024, the "Smokey Gray" uniforms feature an orange 'Tennessee' across the chest.
The orange and white colors worn by the football team were selected by Charles Moore.
Charles Moore was a member of the first Tennessee football team in 1891.
The team colors were inspired by the American Daisy, which grew on The Hill at the university.
Tennessee football players did not wear the orange color until 1922.
The orange color is distinct to the school and is dubbed "UT Orange."
"UT Orange" has been offered by The Home Depot for sale as a licensed paint.
Home games at Neyland Stadium have been described as a "sea of Orange" due to the large number of fans wearing the school color.
The moniker _Big Orange_ derives from the usage of UT Orange.
The official spot color for UT Orange is PMS 151.
The University of Tennessee has had the "Smokey Gray" color since the 1930s.
Tennessee first sported their orange and white checkerboard end zone design in 1964 under coach Dickey.
The checkerboard end zone design remained at Neyland Stadium until artificial turf was installed in 1968.
The checkerboard end zone design was brought back in 1989.
The idea for the checkerboard design was inspired by the checkerboard design around the top of the clock tower at the historic Ayres Hall.
The checkerboard was bordered in orange from 1989 until natural grass replaced the artificial turf in 1994.
The return of natural grass in 1994 brought back the green (or grass colored) border for the checkerboard end zones.
Rocky Top is not the official Tennessee fight song.
_Down the Field_ is the official Tennessee fight song.
Rocky Top is the most popular song in use by the Pride of the Southland Marching Band.
The Pride of the Southland Marching Band began playing Rocky Top in the 1970s.
Rocky Top became popular as a Bluegrass tune by the Osborne Brothers.
Rocky Top became one of Tennessee's state songs in 1982.
Smokey is the mascot of the University of Tennessee sports teams.
Smokey is a Bluetick Coonhound mascot.
Smokey X leads the Vols on the field for football games.
On game weekends, Smokey is cared for by the members of Alpha Gamma Rho's Alpha Kappa chapter.
A costumed mascot, which has won several mascot championships, is present at every Vols game.
Smokey was selected as the mascot for Tennessee after a student poll in 1953.
A contest was held by the Pep Club in 1953 to select a coon hound native to Tennessee.
Rev. Bill Brooks' "Blue Smokey" was chosen as the mascot.
The current Smokey is Smokey XI.
Smokey X was retired at the conclusion of the 2022 season.
Smokey VIII saw a record of 91–22, two SEC titles, and the 1998 National Championship.
Head coach Johnny Majors conceived the idea for the Vol Walk.
The idea for the Vol Walk originated after a 1988 game at Auburn, inspired by the Tiger Walk.
The Vol Walk became an official part of gameday on October 20, 1990, in a Tennessee-Alabama match.
Prior to each home game, the Vols file out of the Neyland-Thompson Sports Complex.
The Vol Walk goes past the Tennessee Volunteers Wall of Fame, down Peyton Manning Pass, and onto Phillip Fulmer Way.
Thousands of fans line the street to shake the players' hands during the Vol Walk.
The Pride of the Southland Band plays Rocky Top during the Vol Walk.
The "T" appears in two special places in Vols history and tradition.
The "T" first appeared on the side of the helmets in 1964, added by coach Doug Dickey.
A rounded "T" came to the helmets in 1968.
Johnny Majors modified the orange helmet stripe to a thicker stripe in 1977.
The Vols _run through the T_ before games.
The "T" for the run-through is formed by the Pride of the Southland marching band.
The base of the "T" is at the entrance to the Tennessee locker room in the north end zone.
Team personnel hold the state flag and the UT flag during the run-through.
Smokey runs onto the field during the run-through.
The entire UT team storms through the "T" to loud cheers and applause.
Coach Dickey brought the "run through the T" tradition to UT in 1965.
From 1965, the Vols' locker room was underneath the East stands.
The team would run through the "T" and turn back to return to their sideline when the locker room was under the East stands.
Beginning in 1983, the team made a left turn inside the "T" and ran toward their former bench on the east sideline.
The locker room was moved from the east sideline to the north end zone in 1983.
On January 24, 2010, it was announced that the Vols would switch their sideline from the east to the west for all home games.
The sideline switch resulted in the Vols making a right turn out of the "T" instead of a left.
The sideline change took effect with Tennessee's first home game of the 2010 season against UT-Martin.
The nickname "Volunteers" (or "Vols") derives from the State of Tennessee's nickname.
Tennessee is known as the "Volunteer State."
Tennessee earned the nickname "Volunteer State" during the War of 1812.
Volunteer soldiers from Tennessee played a prominent role in the War of 1812, especially during the Battle of New Orleans.
Around 200 or more boats normally dock outside Neyland Stadium on the Tennessee River before games.
The Vol Navy fleet was started by former Tennessee broadcaster George Mooney in 1962.
George Mooney started the Vol Navy to avoid traffic around the stadium.
The Vol Navy has grown from one man tying his runabout to a nearby tree and climbing through a wooded area to the stadium.
Many fans arrive several days in advance to socialize as part of the Vol Navy.
The Vols have built a large walkway for fans to safely walk to and from the shoreline.
UT, the University of Pittsburgh, Baylor University, and the University of Washington are the only schools with football stadiums built next to major bodies of water.
As of the end of the 2023 regular season, Tennessee ranks eleventh all-time in won-lost records by percentage.
As of the end of the 2023 regular season, Tennessee ranks tenth all-time in victories.
The all-time record for Tennessee football is 870–415–53 (.670).
At Neyland Stadium, the Vols have an official record of 478–141–17 (.765).
11 additional wins from 2019–20 were vacated by an NCAA Committee on Infractions penalty ruling in July 2023.
One of the vacated victories was in a bowl game.
Tennessee's all-time on-field record is 881–415–53 (.673).
Tennessee's all-time on-field record at Neyland Stadium is 494–142–18 (.770).
The UT football season records are taken from the official record books of the University Athletic Association.
Tennessee's last national championship was in the 1998 college football season.
Tennessee holds the NCAA record for the most consecutive shutout wins with 17.
The consecutive shutout streak started with a Volunteers win against Tennessee-Chattanooga on November 30, 1938.
The consecutive shutout streak ended with a 27–12 loss against Alabama on October 19, 1940.
During the 17-game shutout streak, Tennessee outscored its opponents 479–0.
Tennessee also holds the record for the most consecutive quarters opponents held scoreless, with 71.
Neyland Stadium surrounds Shields–Watkins Field.
Shields–Watkins Field is the official name of the playing surface at Neyland Stadium.
Tennessee boasts the most college football hall of famers in the SEC.
Tennessee has the seventh most college football hall of famers in major college football.
Tennessee has the ninth most college football hall of famers of all college football programs.
Tennessee has 24 college football hall of famers.
Tennessee has retired eight jersey numbers.
Peyton Manning won the Maxwell Award in 1997.
Peyton Manning won the Davey O'Brien Award in 1997.
Peyton Manning won the Johnny Unitas Golden Arm Award in 1997.
Steve DeLong won the Outland Trophy in 1964.
John Henderson won the Outland Trophy in 2000.
Peyton Manning won the Draddy Trophy in 1997.
Michael Munoz won the Draddy Trophy in 2004.
Eric Berry won the Jim Thorpe Award in 2009.
Jalin Hyatt won the Fred Biletnikoff Award in 2022.
Phillip Fulmer won The Home Depot Coach of the Year Award in 1998.
Phillip Fulmer won the Eddie Robinson Coach of the Year Award in 1998.
David Cutcliffe won the Broyles Award in 1998.
John Chavis won the American Football Coaches Association Assistant Coach of the Year Award in 2006.
Phillip Fulmer won the Robert R. Neyland Award in 2009.
From 1992 to 2023, Tennessee played in the East Division of the SEC.
During the 1992-2023 period, Tennessee played each opponent in its division each year.
During the 1992-2023 period, Tennessee also played several teams from the West Division.
In 2024, the SEC expanded the conference to 16 teams.
In 2024, the SEC eliminated its two divisions.
The SEC's expansion and division elimination caused a new scheduling format for the Volunteers.
The SEC announced that teams will play the same opponents in 2025 as in 2024.
For 2025, sites for games will be changed to ensure equal home and away competition over the course of the two seasons.
The SEC is still considering a new format for the future beyond 2025.

