From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Cricket tournament

| !Tournament logo The Ranji Trophy, which the winners get. |
| Administrator(s) | BCCI |
| Cricket format | First-class cricket |
| Tournament format(s) | League and knockout |
|

**********Extracted claims**********

The 1971–72 Ranji Trophy was the 38th season of the Ranji Trophy.
Bombay won the 1971–72 Ranji Trophy.
Bombay defeated Bengal in the final of the 1971–72 Ranji Trophy.
The 1971–72 Ranji Trophy marked Bombay's 14th consecutive title victory.
Bombay secured its 23rd title overall in the Ranji Trophy.
The Board of Control for Cricket in India (BCCI) is the administrator of the Ranji Trophy.
The Ranji Trophy is played in the First-class cricket format.
The Ranji Trophy tournament format includes league and knockout stages.
22 participants competed in the Ranji Trophy season.
Chetan Chauhan of Maharashtra scored the most runs (540) in the 1971–72 Ranji Trophy.
Padmakar Shivalkar of Bombay took the most wickets (41) in the 1971–72 Ranji Trophy.
Bengal was captained in the final by former Indian football captain Chuni Goswami.
In the East Zone, Bengal finished first with 21 points and qualified for the next round.
In the East Zone, Bihar finished second with 20 points.
In the East Zone, Orissa finished third with 5 points.
In the East Zone, Assam finished fourth with 3 points.
In the South Zone, Hyderabad finished first with 30 points and qualified for the next round.
In the South Zone, Mysore finished second with 24 points.
In the South Zone, Tamil Nadu finished third with 22 points.
In the South Zone, Kerala finished fourth with 8 points.
In the South Zone, Andhra finished fifth with 0 points.
In the North Zone, Punjab finished first with 16 points and qualified for the next round.
In the North Zone, Railways finished second with 15 points.
In the North Zone, Delhi finished third with 15 points.
In the North Zone, Haryana finished fourth with 3 points.
