**********Extracted claims**********

_Cyber Citizen Shockman 2: A New Menace_ is a 1991 video game.
The Japanese title for the game is _Kaizō Chōjin Shubibinman 2: Aratanaru Teki_.
The game was developed by Winds.
The game was published by Masaya Games, NCS Corporation, Hudson Soft, and Ratalaika Games.
The game was released on TurboGrafx-16, Nintendo Switch, PlayStation 4, PlayStation 5, Xbox One, and Xbox Series X/S.
The TurboGrafx-16 version was released in Japan on April 27, 1991.
The TurboGrafx-16 version was released in North America on November 22, 1992.
The Nintendo Switch, PlayStation 4/5, Xbox One, and Xbox Series X/S versions were released worldwide on September 22, 2023.
The game's genre is platform.
The game supports up to 2 players.
The game was released in the United States in 1992 as _Shockman_.
_Cyber Citizen Shockman 2: A New Menace_ is the only game in the _Cyber Citizen Shockman_ series to have a contemporary release outside Japan.
The plot of _A New Menace_ takes place approximately two years after the original _Cyber Citizen Shockman_.
Arnold, known as Tasuke in the Japanese version, works as a cook in a local restaurant.
Sonya, known as Kyapiko, remains a student.
Doc constantly suspected another invasion was coming after the fall of the Dark Skull.
Arnold and Sonya conducted searches for new invaders but did not find anything before Doc was kidnapped.
An alien empire led by Ryo plans to take over the world.
Two other villains, Jeeta (Bēta) and Mue (Myū), have appeared with powers similar to Arnold and Sonya, and are described as dark Shockmen.
Arnold and Sonya must rescue Doc.
Arnold and Sonya must stop Ryo from taking over the world.
Arnold and Sonya must avoid being killed by Jeeta and Mue.
_A New Menace_ plays differently from the previous title.
Swordplay has been eliminated from the gameplay.
Swordplay is replaced by a ranged weapon similar to Mega Man's Mega Buster.
The world map, upgrades, and hostages have been removed from the game.
The game includes two side-scrolling shooter stages.
Jump control has been altered in the game.
The game was re-released on the Wii Virtual Console worldwide throughout 2007.
The game was released in the 2010s for the Wii U Virtual Console.
The game received a release for the iPhone in 2011 through an app called "PC Engine Gamebox".
The "PC Engine Gamebox" app included several other PC Engine titles.
Ratalaika Games re-released the game on September 22, 2023.
The game received average reviews from critics.
The Japanese publication _Micom BASIC Magazine_ ranked the game third in popularity in its July 1991 issue.
The game received a score of 22.63 out of 30 in a 1993 readers' poll conducted by _PC Engine Fan_.
The game ranked at the number 113 spot among PC Engine titles in a 1993 readers' poll by _PC Engine Fan_.
_Eurogamer_ gave the game a score of 6/10.
_Famitsu_ gave the game a score of 21/40.
_Gekkan PC Engine_ gave the game a score of 80/100.
_Génération 4_ gave the game a score of 70%.
_Joystick_ gave the game a score of 83%.
_Marukatsu PC Engine_ gave the game a score of 27/40.
_Nintendo Life_ gave the game a score of 6/10.
_Play Time_ gave the game a score of 44%.

