Olu Jacobs starred in _The Witches and the Grinnygog_.
James Harpham composed music for _The Witches and the Grinnygog_.
The country of origin for _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ is the United Kingdom.
The original language of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ is English.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ has one season.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ has six episodes.
Anna Home was the executive producer for _The Witches and the Grinnygog_.
The running time for each episode of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ is 25 minutes.
TVS was the production company for _The Witches and the Grinnygog_.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ aired on the ITV network.
The story of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ was adapted from a book titled _The Witches and the Grinnygog_.
Dorothy Edwards wrote the book _The Witches and the Grinnygog_.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ was re-broadcast in the US.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ was re-broadcast in Canada.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ was re-broadcast in New Zealand.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ was re-broadcast in Israel.
A strange statue, known as the Grinnygog, fell off the back of a truck.
The truck was carrying stones from an old church for relocation.
A woman discovered the Grinnygog on her way home from the store.
The woman took the statue home.
The woman gave the statue to her elderly father as a garden ornament.
The family possessing the Grinnygog began to experience strange feelings of euphoria.
The family possessing the Grinnygog began to experience a desire to participate in folk magic rituals.
A nervous, "other-worldly" child began to be seen around the town.
The nervous child appeared lost and frightened.
Three eccentric older women arrived in the town.
The three women appeared to be searching for something.
The three women brought a "daughter" of one of the women with them.
The "daughter" was later revealed to be a mannequin.
The mannequin appeared to be walking by itself at one point.
The three women are witches from England's 17th century.
The witches escaped a witch-hunt by slipping through time into the modern age.
The Grinnygog was an image of the Horned God.
The Grinnygog had lain dormant until its removal from the church.
The Grinnygog summoned the witches to the present day.
One of the witches is looking for her daughter.
The daughter was separated during a witch-burning held hundreds of years ago in the town.
The daughter escaped by slipping through time.
The daughter slipped through time only intermittently.
The daughter is lost without her mother.
A group of children worked out the presence of the witches and their relationship to the Grinnygog.
The children investigated their town's local history.
The children discovered that the town must make amends for the ancient injustice of witch-burning.
An old manuscript revealed one clue.
A town elder wrote the old manuscript.
The town elder heard of the witch-burning from his elderly servant.
The town elder wrote an account of the event in a journal.
The town elder died before revealing the final clue about the escaped witches.
The arrival of an African witch doctor named Mr. Alabaster was another clue.
Mr. Alabaster was seeking out the witches to help them.
The story ends with the witches reunited with the daughter.
The witches were taken away by Mr. Alabaster.
The Grinnygog was taken away with the witches and Mr. Alabaster.
The TV series was filmed in and around Titchfield, Hampshire.
The TV series was filmed in and around Bishop's Waltham, Hampshire.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ was shown on Nickelodeon in the United States.
_The Witches and the Grinnygog_ was shown as part of the anthology series _The Third Eye_.
Episode 1 of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ aired on 14 November 1983.
Episode 2 of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ aired on 21 November 1983.
Episode 3 of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ aired on 28 November 1983.
Episode 4 of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ aired on 5 December 1983.
Episode 5 of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ aired on 12 December 1983.
Episode 6 of _The Witches and the Grinnygog_ aired on 19 December 1983.
Paul Curtis played Jimmy Firkettle.
Zoe Loftin played Essie Firkettle.
Adam Woodyatt played Dave Firkettle.
Giles Harper played Colin Sogood.
Heidi Mayo played Nan Sogood.
Sheila Grant played Mrs. Ems.
Anna Wing played Edie Possett.
Patricia Hayes played Miss Bendybones.
Eva Griffith played Margaret "Daisy" Ems.
Robert Swann played Reverend Anthony Sogood.
John Barrard played Granddad Adams.
Jane Wood played Mrs. Firkettle.
Olu Jacobs played Twebele Alabaster.
Anne Dyson played Miss Possett.
Liam O'Callaghan played Patrick Flanagan.
Gary Hailes played Flanagan's mate.
Alan Rowe played Major Gilmour.
George Malpas played Old Peggy.
Hilda Fenemore played Mrs. Featherly.
Carolyn Courage played the News Interviewer.
Heather Tobias played the Shop Customer.
Tariq Yunus played the Bus Driver.
A song is featured in episode 4 of the series.
The song is sung by the Granddad and the children.
The lyrics of the song are taken from the original book by Dorothy Edwards.
The book by Dorothy Edwards refers to the ‘la-las’ in the song as being in place of forgotten words.
No explanation of the meaning of the song is given in the TV adaptation.
The book contains more lines for the song than the TV adaptation.
The TV adaptation's song is sung to a memorable, evocative melody.
The melody of the song is repeated during the end credits.
The lyrics of the song sung in episode 4 are: “Great Hern would horn, ‘Fore I was born. Flash green and grin the round, Up besom fly, Tra-la-la la-la-la”.
There has been no domestic commercial release of the series on any format in the UK.
The lack of release is possibly due to ongoing rights issues.
The production company, TVS, dropped out of the ITV network in 1992.
TVS subsequently went through a number of take-overs.
The problem of no commercial release affects the majority of the TVS programme archive.
Much of the original production paperwork and sales documentation has been lost.
The Walt Disney Company are the current owners of the TVS programme archive.
The Walt Disney Company appears to have little or no interest in these programmes.

