**********Extracted claims**********

Interleukin 7 (IL-7) is a protein.
The _IL7_ gene encodes Interleukin 7 in humans.
IL-7 is a hematopoietic growth factor.
IL-7 is secreted by stromal cells in the bone marrow and thymus.
Keratinocytes, follicular dendritic cells, hepatocytes, neurons, and epithelial cells also produce IL-7.
Normal lymphocytes do not produce IL-7.
Autocrine production of IL-7 cytokine by T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) can be involved in the oncogenic development of T-ALL.
Autocrine production of IL-7 cytokine by T-ALL offers novel insights into T-ALL spreading.
The three-dimensional structure of IL-7 in complex with the ectodomain of IL-7 receptor has been determined.
X-ray diffraction was used to determine the three-dimensional structure of IL-7 in complex with the ectodomain of IL-7 receptor.
IL-7 is a cytokine important for B and T cell development.
IL-7 and hepatocyte growth factor (HGF) form a heterodimer.
The IL-7 and HGF heterodimer functions as a pre-pro-B cell growth-stimulating factor.
IL-7 is a cofactor for V(D)J rearrangement of the T cell receptor beta (TCRß) during early T cell development.
Knockout studies in mice suggest that IL-7 plays an essential role in lymphoid cell survival.
IL-7 binds to the IL-7 receptor.
The IL-7 receptor is a heterodimer consisting of Interleukin-7 receptor alpha and common gamma chain receptor.
Binding of IL-7 to its receptor results in a cascade of signals.
These signals are important for T-cell development within the thymus and survival within the periphery.
Knockout mice genetically lacking the IL-7 receptor exhibit thymic atrophy.
Knockout mice genetically lacking the IL-7 receptor exhibit arrest of T-cell development at the double positive stage.
Knockout mice genetically lacking the IL-7 receptor exhibit severe lymphopenia.
Administration of IL-7 to mice results in an increase in recent thymic emigrants.
Administration of IL-7 to mice results in increases in B and T cells.
Administration of IL-7 to mice results in increased recovery of T cells after cyclophosphamide administration.
Administration of IL-7 to mice results in increased recovery of T cells after bone marrow transplantation.
IL-7 promotes hematological malignancies.
Hematological malignancies promoted by IL-7 include acute lymphoblastic leukemia and T cell lymphoma.
Elevated levels of IL-7 have been detected in the plasma of HIV-infected patients.
IL-7 as an immunotherapy agent has been examined in pre-clinical animal studies and human clinical trials.
Recombinant IL-7 has been safely administered to patients in several phase I and II clinical trials.
A human study of IL-7 in cancer patients demonstrated that its administration can transiently disrupt the homeostasis of both CD8+ and CD4+ T cells.
The administration of IL-7 in cancer patients leads to a commensurate decrease in the percentage of CD4+CD25+Foxp3+ T regulatory cells.
No objective cancer regression was observed in a human study of IL-7 in cancer patients.
A dose limiting toxicity (DLT) was not reached in a human study of IL-7 in cancer patients due to the development of neutralizing antibodies against the recombinant cytokine.
Associated with antiretroviral therapy, IL-7 administration decreased local and systemic inflammations in patients who had incomplete T-cell reconstitution.

