**********Extracted claims**********

_Panulirus echinatus_ is a species of crustacean.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is also known as the brown spidy lobster.
_Panulirus echinatus_ lives on rocky reefs in the tropical western Atlantic Ocean.
_Panulirus echinatus_ lives on rocky reefs in the central Atlantic Islands.
The binomial name of the species is _Panulirus echinatus_.
Smith, 1869, is the authority for the species _Panulirus echinatus_.
The conservation status of _Panulirus echinatus_ is "Least Concern".
The "Least Concern" status for _Panulirus echinatus_ is according to IUCN 3.1.
_Panulirus echinatus_ belongs to the Kingdom Animalia.
_Panulirus echinatus_ belongs to the Phylum Arthropoda.
_Panulirus echinatus_ belongs to the Class Malacostraca.
_Panulirus echinatus_ belongs to the Order Decapoda.
_Panulirus echinatus_ belongs to the Suborder Pleocyemata.
_Panulirus echinatus_ belongs to the Family Palinuridae.
_Panulirus echinatus_ belongs to the Genus _Panulirus_.
_Panulirus echinatus_ has no pincer-like chelae on its front walking legs.
_Panulirus echinatus_ has just two large spines on the antennular plate, which is in front of its carapace.
The exopod of the third feeding appendage of _Panulirus echinatus_ is reduced.
The exopod of the third feeding appendage of _Panulirus echinatus_ bears no flagellum.
The basic color of _Panulirus echinatus_ is brown.
_Panulirus echinatus_ has large white rounded spots.
The antennules of _Panulirus echinatus_ are brown.
The limbs of _Panulirus echinatus_ are brown.
The antennules of _Panulirus echinatus_ have longitudinal white or yellow markings.
The limbs of _Panulirus echinatus_ have longitudinal white or yellow markings.
The average size of _Panulirus echinatus_ varies over different parts of its range.
Males of _Panulirus echinatus_ grow to a carapace length of about 19 cm (7.5 in).
Females of _Panulirus echinatus_ grow to a carapace length of about 15 cm (6 in).
Males of _Panulirus echinatus_ are generally more numerous than females.
Males of _Panulirus echinatus_ are generally larger than females.
Males of _Panulirus echinatus_ are generally heavier than females.
Females of _Panulirus echinatus_ feed less during the breeding season.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is found off the coasts of northern Brazil.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is found on the Atlantic Islands of Cape Verde.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is found on Saint Helena.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is found on Ascension Island.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is found on Tristan da Cunha.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is found on the Canary Islands.
The depth range of _Panulirus echinatus_ is about 35 m (115 ft).
_Panulirus echinatus_ is seldom found deeper than 25 m (82 ft).
_Panulirus echinatus_ lives on rocks.
_Panulirus echinatus_ lives among boulders.
_Panulirus echinatus_ hides in cracks and fissures during the day.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is a nocturnal feeder.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is a generalist feeder.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is an opportunistic browser.
Examination of stomach contents of _Panulirus echinatus_ caught on the Saint Peter and Saint Paul Archipelago shows that fish is the largest dietary item.
Crustaceans form part of the diet of _Panulirus echinatus_.
The green alga _Caulerpa racemosa_ is part of the diet of _Panulirus echinatus_.
_Caulerpa racemosa_ is common in the Saint Peter and Saint Paul Archipelago study area.
Calcareous algae scraped off the rock surface are consumed by _Panulirus echinatus_.
Calcareous algae are an important source of calcium for the formation of the hard shell of _Panulirus echinatus_.
Rock fragments found in the stomach of _Panulirus echinatus_ are likely ingested accidentally.
Females of _Panulirus echinatus_ carry their egg mass on their abdomens, under their tails.
Females of _Panulirus echinatus_ carry their egg mass for several months until the eggs hatch.
During the egg-carrying period, females of _Panulirus echinatus_ are likely to hide away more in crevices.
During the egg-carrying period, females of _Panulirus echinatus_ spend less time feeding.
Males of _Panulirus echinatus_ do not change their feeding behavior during the breeding season.
The larvae of _Panulirus echinatus_ are planktonic.
A late-stage phyllosoma larva taken in mid-ocean has been identified as belonging to the species _Panulirus echinatus_.
The identification of the larva was achieved by mitochondrial DNA analysis.
_Panulirus echinatus_ has a wide distribution.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is very common within its range.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is harvested in most areas where it is found.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is fished commercially in Saint Helena.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is fished commercially in the Cape Verde Islands.
The population trend of _Panulirus echinatus_ is unclear.
The population of _Panulirus echinatus_ has likely been reduced over the years due to fishing pressure.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is likely resilient.
_Panulirus echinatus_ is not likely to suffer local extinctions.
The high fecundity of the female _Panulirus echinatus_ contributes to the species' resilience.
The International Union for Conservation of Nature has assessed the conservation status of _Panulirus echinatus_ as "least concern".

