From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Subtype of the influenza A virus

| Influenza A virus subtype H7N9 |
| --- |
| Virus classification!Edit this classification |
| (unranked): | Virus |
| Realm: | _Riboviria_ |
| Kingdom: | _Orthornav

**********Extracted claims**********

Influenza A virus subtype H7N9 (A/H7N9) is a subtype of the influenza A virus.
Influenza A virus subtype H7N9 predominantly causes influenza in birds.
Influenza A virus subtype H7N9 is enzootic (maintained in the population) in many bird populations.
The A/H7N9 virus can spread rapidly through poultry flocks.
The A/H7N9 virus can spread rapidly among wild birds.
The A/H7N9 virus can infect humans exposed to infected birds.
The A/H7N9 virus is shed in the saliva, mucus, and feces of infected birds.
Other infected animals may shed bird flu viruses in respiratory secretions and other body fluids.
Symptoms of A/H7N9 influenza vary according to the strain of virus underlying the infection.
Symptoms of A/H7N9 influenza vary according to the species of bird affected.
Symptoms of A/H7N9 influenza vary according to the species of mammal affected.
Classification as Low Pathogenic Avian Influenza (LPAI) or High Pathogenic Avian Influenza (HPAI) is based on the severity of symptoms in domestic chickens.
Classification as LPAI or HPAI does not predict the severity of symptoms in humans.
Chickens infected with LPAI A/H7N9 virus display mild symptoms or are asymptomatic.
HPAI A/H7N9 causes serious breathing difficulties in domestic chickens.
HPAI A/H7N9 causes a significant drop in egg production in domestic chickens.
HPAI A/H7N9 causes sudden death in domestic chickens.
A/H7N9 influenza infection is rare in mammals, including humans.
Human A/H7N9 influenza infection can usually be traced to close contact with infected poultry.
Human A/H7N9 influenza infection can usually be traced to contaminated material such as feces.
Symptoms of A/H7N9 infection in mammals vary from mild to severe.
Symptoms of A/H7N9 infection in mammals include fever.
Symptoms of A/H7N9 infection in mammals include diarrhea.
Symptoms of A/H7N9 infection in mammals include cough.
The disease caused by A/H7N9 infection in mammals can often be fatal.
The A/H7N9 virus is considered to be continually present in wild aquatic birds.
Wild aquatic birds may carry the A/H7N9 virus over large distances during their migration.
The first known case of A/H7N9 influenza infecting humans was reported in March 2013 in China.
A/H7N9 cases continued to be recorded in poultry in China for at least five years after March 2013.
A/H7N9 cases continued to be recorded in humans in China for at least five years after March 2013.
Between February 2013 and February 2019, 1,568 confirmed human cases of A(H7N9) were reported in China.
Between February 2013 and February 2019, 616 deaths were associated with the A(H7N9) outbreak in China.
The A/H7N9 virus was initially low pathogenic to poultry.
Around 2017, a highly pathogenic strain of A/H7N9 developed and became dominant.
The A/H7N9 outbreak in China has been partially contained by a program of poultry vaccination.
The poultry vaccination program in China commenced in 2017.
Following the 2017 vaccination campaign, the number of A/H7N9 outbreaks in poultry dropped significantly.
Following the 2017 vaccination campaign, the number of human A/H7N9 cases dropped significantly.
In humans, symptoms and mortality for both LPAI and HPAI A/H7N9 strains have been similar.
No human H7N9 infections have been reported since February 2019.
The A/H7N9 virus is still circulating in poultry, particularly in laying hens.
The A/H7N9 virus has demonstrated antigenic drift to evade vaccines.
The A/H7N9 virus remains a potential threat to the poultry industry.
The A/H7N9 virus remains a potential threat to public health.
As of May 2022, a total of 1568 confirmed A(H7N9) human infections had been reported.
As of May 2022, 616 deaths had been reported from A(H7N9) human infections.
As of May 2022, the case fatality ratio (CFR) for A(H7N9) human infections was 39%.
Bird-adapted A/H7N9 transmits relatively easily from poultry to humans.
Human-to-human transmission of A/H7N9 is rare.
The ability of A/H7N9 to cross the species barrier renders it a potential pandemic threat.
H7N9 is an enveloped negative-sense RNA virus.
H7N9 has a segmented genome.
Influenza viruses have a relatively high mutation rate.
The high mutation rate is characteristic of RNA viruses.
The segmentation of the influenza virus genome facilitates genetic recombination by reassortment.
Genetic recombination by reassortment occurs in hosts infected with two different strains of influenza viruses at the same time.
Through a combination of mutation and genetic reassortment, the virus can evolve to acquire new characteristics.
The virus can evolve to evade host immunity.
The virus can occasionally jump from one species of host to another.
A classification system for avian virus strains was devised in 1981.
The 1981 classification system divided avian virus strains as either highly pathogenic or low pathogenic.
Highly pathogenic strains may require vigorous control measures.
The test for pathogenicity is based solely on the effect on chickens.
A virus strain is classified as highly pathogenic avian influenza (HPAI) if 75% or more of chickens die after being deliberately infected with it.
Low pathogenic avian influenza (LPAI) is the alternative classification.
The classification system for avian virus strains has been modified to take into account the structure of the virus' haemagglutinin protein.
Other species of birds, especially water birds, can become infected with HPAI virus without experiencing severe symptoms.
HPAI can be spread over large distances by infected birds.
The exact symptoms of HPAI in birds depend on the species of bird.
The exact symptoms of HPAI in birds depend on the strain of virus.
Classification of an avian virus strain as HPAI or LPAI does not predict how serious the disease might be if it infects humans.
Classification of an avian virus strain as HPAI or LPAI does not predict how serious the disease might be if it infects other mammals.
Since 2006, the World Organization for Animal Health requires all LPAI H5 and H7 detections to be reported.
LPAI H5 and H7 strains have the potential to mutate into highly pathogenic strains.
Some species of wild aquatic birds act as natural asymptomatic carriers of a large variety of influenza A viruses.
Wild aquatic birds can spread influenza A viruses over large distances in their annual migration.
Symptoms of avian influenza in birds vary according to the strain of virus underlying the infection.
Symptoms of avian influenza in birds vary according to the species of bird affected.
Symptoms of influenza in birds may include swollen head.
Symptoms of influenza in birds may include watery eyes.
Symptoms of influenza in birds may include unresponsiveness.
Symptoms of influenza in birds may include lack of coordination.
Symptoms of influenza in birds may include respiratory distress such as sneezing or gurgling.
Humans can only become infected with avian influenza, including A/H7N9, after prolonged close contact with infected birds.
Humans can only become infected with avian influenza, including A/H7N9, after prolonged close contact with contaminated environments.
Other mammals can only become infected with avian influenza, including A/H7N9, after prolonged close contact with infected birds.
Other mammals can only become infected with avian influenza, including A/H7N9, after prolonged close contact with contaminated environments.
As of February 2024, there have been very few instances of human-to-human transmission of A/H7N9.
As of February 2024, each human-to-human A/H7N9 outbreak has been limited to a few people.
All subtypes of avian Influenza A have the potential to cross the species barrier into humans.
H5N1 and H7N9 are considered the biggest threats among avian Influenza A subtypes for crossing the species barrier into humans.
Prior to 2013, A/H7N9 had been isolated only in birds.
Outbreaks of A/H7N9 in birds were reported in the Netherlands prior to 2013.
Outbreaks of A/H7N9 in birds were reported in Japan prior to 2013.
Outbreaks of A/H7N9 in birds were reported in the United States prior to 2013.
Until the 2013 outbreak in China, no human infections with A/H7N9 had been reported.
A significant outbreak of A/H7N9 started in March 2013 in China.
The March 2013 A/H7N9 outbreak in China affected 18 humans with severe influenza.
Six of the 18 affected humans in China in March 2013 subsequently died.
A low pathogenic strain of A/H7N9 was discovered circulating among chickens in China during the 2013 outbreak.
All people affected in the March 2013 China A/H7N9 outbreak had been exposed in poultry markets.
Further cases among humans and poultry in mainland China continued to be identified sporadically throughout 2013.
A peak in A/H7N9 cases occurred around the Chinese New Year festival season (January and February) in early 2014.
The 2014 peak in A/H7N9 cases was attributed to the seasonal surge in poultry production.
Infections among humans and poultry continued in China during the years following 2014.
Peaks in A/H7N9 infections occurred around the Chinese New Year during the years following 2014.
In 2016, a highly pathogenic strain of A/H7N9 emerged in China that was highly pathogenic to chickens.
Genetic characterization shows the "Asian lineage" strain of avian influenza A/H7N9 was not related to A/H7N9 strains previously identified in Europe.
Genetic characterization shows the "Asian lineage" strain of avian influenza A/H7N9 was not related to A/H7N9 strains previously identified in North America.
The new Asian lineage strain resulted from the recombination of genes between several parent viruses.
The parent viruses for the Asian lineage strain were noted in poultry and wild birds in Asia.
The H7 gene of the Asian lineage strain is most closely related to sequences found in samples from ducks in Zhejiang province in 2011.
The N9 gene of the Asian lineage strain was closely related to isolated wild ducks in South Korea in 2011.
Other genes of the Asian lineage strain resembled samples collected in Beijing and Shanghai in 2012.
The genes of the Asian lineage strain were carried along the East Asian flyway by wild birds during their annual migration.
The genetic characteristics of Asian lineage A/H7N9 virus are of particular concern due to their pandemic potential.
The Asian lineage A/H7N9 virus is able to infect humans relatively easily.
The Asian lineage A/H7N9 virus does not sustain human-to-human transmission.
If the Asian lineage A/H7N9 virus were to acquire the ability to transmit easily between humans, there is potential for a severe epidemic.
If the Asian lineage A/H7N9 virus were to acquire the ability to transmit easily between humans, there is potential for a severe pandemic.
Outbreaks of avian influenza A(H7N9) occurred in poultry in the USA in early 2017.
The A(H7N9) strain in the early 2017 USA outbreaks was of North American origin.
The North American origin strain of A(H7N9) is unrelated to the Asian lineage H7N9 associated with human infections in China.
In May 2024, an HPAI A/H7N9 was detected on a poultry farm in Terang, Australia.
The poultry farm in Terang, Australia, housed 151,894 birds.
In the May 2024 outbreak in Terang, Australia, 14,000 birds were clinically affected.
An H7N9 outbreak in the US was confirmed on March 13, 2025.
The March 2025 outbreak occurred on a commercial broiler breeder chicken farm in Noxubee, Mississippi.
The Noxubee, Mississippi, farm housed 47,654 chickens.
All 47,654 chickens on the Noxubee, Mississippi, farm were euthanized as a precautionary measure.
The euthanization was done to prevent further spread of the virus.
The continuing evolution of the A/H7N9 virus makes further vaccine development difficult.
The antigenic drift of the A/H7N9 virus makes further vaccine development difficult.
Any significant outbreak of A/H7N9 could involve a completely new strain.
A number of human vaccines targeting A/H7N9 have been trialled.
Human vaccine trials for A/H7N9 have been conducted in relatively small groups of subjects.
In the event of an A/H7N9 outbreak, a candidate vaccine virus would be distributed to manufacturers.

