Title: Irish National War Memorial Gardens
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irish_National_War_Memorial_Gardens
Content: Jump to content

Coordinates: !53°20′38″N6°19′01″W﻿ / ﻿53.3440°N 6.3170°W﻿ / 53.3440; -6.3170

From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

World War One memorial in Dublin, Ireland

Not to be confused with Garden of Remembrance (Dublin) "Garden of Remembrance 

**********Extracted claims**********

The Irish National War Memorial Gardens is an Irish war memorial located in Islandbridge, Dublin.
The memorial is dedicated to the memory of the 49,400 Irish soldiers who died in the Great War (1914–1918).
A total of 206,000 Irishmen served in the British forces alone during the Great War.
The Memorial Gardens also commemorate all other Irish men and women who served, fought, and died in Irish regiments of the Allied armies (British, Canadian, Australian, New Zealand, South African, and United States armies) in support of the Triple Entente's war effort against the Central Powers.
In 1914, Ireland was part of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland.
Many Irishmen joined the British armed forces in 1914.
The British Army had eight regiments that were raised in Ireland.
A trust fund was created on July 17, 1919, after a meeting of over 100 representatives from all parts of Ireland.
The trust fund was created to consider plans and designs for a permanent memorial to commemorate all Irish men and women killed in the First World War.
A general committee was formed in November 1924 to pursue proposals for a site in Dublin.
On March 28, 1927, Merrion Square or St Stephen's Green were proposed as sites for the memorial.
W. T. Cosgrave, president of the Irish Free State Executive Council, appointed Cecil Lavery to set up a "War Memorial Committee".
Cosgrave suggested the current site, known at the time as "Longmeadows Estates", to Andrew Jameson on December 9, 1930.
The "Longmeadows Estates" is about 60 acres (24 ha) in extent.
The "Longmeadows Estates" stretches parallel along the south bank of the River Liffey from Islandbridge towards Chapelizod.
Cosgrave's proposal was adopted by the committee on December 16, 1931.
General Sir William Hickie stated that the memorial is an all-Ireland one.
The Irish Government sanctioned a generous gift in an eleven-paragraph agreement with the committee on December 12, 1933.
The Dublin City Council Office of Public Works (OPW) began work with 164 men during 1932.
Éamon de Valera's government recognized the motives of the memorial and made valuable state contributions to it.
The cabinet approved that work on the memorial be divided 50% between British and Irish WWI ex-servicemen.
A completion certificate from the Office of Public Works for the memorial was finally issued in January 1938.
An official opening for the memorial was agreed for July 30, 1939, but was postponed.
No official opening ever happened for the memorial.
The first public event in the gardens took place in 1940 for Armistice Day celebrations.
Sir Edwin Lutyens designed the memorial.
The memorial is outstanding among the many war memorials Lutyens created throughout the world.
Lutyens found the memorial site to be glorious.
The sunken Garden of Remembrance surrounds a Stone of Remembrance.
The Stone of Remembrance is made of Irish granite.
The Stone of Remembrance weighs seven and a half tons.
The dimensions of the Stone of Remembrance are identical to First World War memorials found throughout the world.
The Stone of Remembrance is aligned with the Great Cross and Central Avenue.
The memorial lies about three kilometers from the center of Dublin.
The memorial grounds gradually slope upwards towards Kilmainham Hill.
Old chronicles describe Kilmainham Hill as the camping place of Brian Boru and his army prior to the Battle of Clontarf on April 23, 1014.
The memorial was among the last to be erected to the memory of those who sacrificed their lives in World War I.
Canada's National War Memorial was opened in 1939.
The memorial is "the symbol of Remembrance in memory of a Nation's sacrifice."
The memorial's layout includes a central sunken rose garden composed by a committee of horticulturalists.
The memorial's layout includes various terraces, pergolas, lawns, and avenues lined with impressive parkland trees.
The memorial includes two pairs of book rooms in granite, representing the four provinces of Ireland.
The book rooms contain illuminated volumes recording the names of all the dead.
A domed temple stands at the north of the gardens, overlooking the River Liffey.
The temple marks the beginning of the avenue leading gently upwards to the steps containing the Stone of Remembrance.
The floor of the temple contains an extract from "War Sonnet II: Safety" by Rupert Brooke.
Workers for the memorial's construction were drawn from the unemployed, with 50 percent being former World War I ex-British Army and 50 percent being ex-Irish Army men.
The use of mechanical equipment was restricted during construction to provide as much work as possible.
Granite blocks of 7 and 8 tonnes from Ballyknockan and Donnelly's quarry in Barnacullia were manhandled into place with primitive tackles of poles and ropes.
Commemorations by Irish British Armed Forces veterans and families of those killed in the Great War took place at the site in the late 1940s and 1950s.
The garden was subject to two Irish Republican paramilitary attacks.
On Christmas night 1956, a bomb was placed at the base of the Stone of Remembrance and memorial cross and detonated.
The County Wicklow quarried granite of the memorial withstood the 1956 blast with little damage.
Another attempt was made to bring the memorial down with a bomb detonation in October 1958.
The 1958 bomb detonation failed to bring the memorial down, resulting in superficial damage.
A lack of financing from the government caused the site to fall into dilapidation and vandalism over subsequent decades.
By the late 1970s, the garden had become a site for caravans and animals of the Irish Traveller community.
The Dublin Corporation's refuse disposal office used the garden as a rubbish dump for the city's waste by the late 1970s.
Fifty years of storms and the elements had left their mark on the garden, with structural damage unrepaired to parts of its ornamentation.
Economic and cultural shifts in Ireland in the mid-1980s facilitated a regeneration of urban decay in Dublin.
A project of restoration work was undertaken by the Office of Public Works, co-funded by the National War Memorial Committee.
The fully restored gardens were re-opened to the public on September 10, 1988.
The gardens were formally dedicated by representatives of the four main churches of Ireland in 1988.
An architectural competition was launched in November 2018 to design a bridge to be built over the River Liffey.
The proposed bridge would provide access to the gardens from Chapelizod Road.
The proposed bridge is in partial fulfillment of Lutyens' original plan, which included a bridge over the river.
An architectural design for the bridge was selected in May 2018.
A contractor was appointed in July 2025 to carry out the bridge works.
A state commemoration for the 90th anniversary of the Battle of the Somme took place on July 1, 2006.
President of Ireland Mary McAleese and Taoiseach Bertie Ahern attended the 2006 commemoration.
Queen Elizabeth II and President of Ireland Mary McAleese laid wreaths to honor Ireland's dead of World War I and World War II at a ceremony in the garden on May 18, 2011.
The 2011 event was during the first state visit of a British monarch to the Irish Republic.
A state ceremony to mark the centenary of the Battle of the Somme took place within the gardens on July 9, 2016.
Taoiseach Enda Kenny attended the 2016 ceremony.
President of Ireland Michael D. Higgins laid a wreath at the 2016 ceremony to honor the soldiers of Ireland who lost their lives during the Battle of the Somme.
The 8 illuminated volumes of *Ireland's Memorial Records 1914-1918* are housed in granite paved pergolas surrounding the garden.
The *Records* document the names of the dead.
Each page of the *Records* is illustrated on four sides with artwork by Harry Clarke.
The *Records* are displayed in cases designed by Lutyens.
A wooden cross of oak, known as the Ginchy Cross, is housed in the same building as the *Records*.
The Ginchy Cross was built by the 16th (Irish) Division.
The Ginchy Cross was originally erected on the Somme.
The Ginchy Cross commemorates 4,354 men of the 16th (Irish) Division who died in two engagements.
Three granite replicas of the Ginchy Cross are erected at locations liberated by Irish divisions: Guillemont and Messines-Wytschaete in Belgium, and Thessaloniki in Greece.
The Irish National War Memorial Gardens are managed by the Government's Office of Public Works (OPW).
The gardens are managed in conjunction with the National War Memorial Committee.
A further Great War Irish national memorial, the Island of Ireland Peace Park, was jointly opened in 1998.
The Island of Ireland Peace Park is located in Messines, Flanders, Belgium.
Mary McAleese, President of Ireland, Queen Elizabeth II, and Albert II, King of the Belgians, jointly opened the Island of Ireland Peace Park.
A small stream, commonly known as the Creosote Stream, emerges and flows into the River Liffey at the western edge of the gardens.
The Creosote Stream rises west of Inchicore railway works.
The Creosote Stream flows underneath the site to emerge close to the Liffey on the grounds of the War Memorial Gardens.
The Creosote Stream received its name from pollutants that used to leach into it from the railway works.

