From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Smooth muscle tissue in the walls of blood vessels

| Vascular smooth muscle |
| --- |
| ! Diagram showing the location of vascular smooth muscle cells |
| ! Vascular smooth muscle cells, isolated fr

**********Extracted claims**********

Vascular smooth muscle is a type of smooth muscle.
Vascular smooth muscle is found in the walls of blood vessels.
Vascular smooth muscle comprises much of the walls in some types of blood vessels.
Capillaries have no smooth muscle.
Vascular smooth muscle is primarily innervated by the sympathetic nervous system.
The sympathetic nervous system innervates vascular smooth muscle through alpha-1, alpha-2, and beta-2 adrenergic receptors.
Norepinephrine (NE) is the main endogenous agonist of alpha-1, alpha-2, and beta-2 adrenergic receptors in vascular smooth muscle.
Alpha-1 receptors cause vasoconstriction when norepinephrine binds to them.
Activation of alpha-1 receptors decreases the diameter of blood vessels.
Alpha-1 receptors are activated in response to shock or low blood pressure.
Activation of alpha-1 receptors acts as a defensive reaction to restore normal blood pressure.
Antagonists of alpha-1 receptors cause vasodilation.
Antagonists of alpha-1 receptors cause a decrease in vascular smooth muscle tone.
Antagonists of alpha-1 receptors cause an increase in vessel diameter.
Antagonists of alpha-1 receptors cause a decrease in blood pressure.
Agonists of alpha-2 receptors in the vascular smooth muscle lead to vasoconstriction.
Agonism of beta-2 receptors causes vasodilation.
Agonism of beta-2 receptors causes low blood pressure.
The effect of beta-2 receptor activation is opposite to the effect resulting from activation of alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors in vascular smooth muscle cells.
Vascular smooth muscle contracts or relaxes to change the volume of blood vessels.
Vascular smooth muscle contracts or relaxes to change the local blood pressure.
The contraction or relaxation of vascular smooth muscle is a mechanism responsible for redistributing blood within the body.
Blood is redistributed to areas where it is needed, such as areas with temporarily enhanced oxygen consumption.
The main function of vascular smooth muscle tone is to regulate the caliber of the blood vessels in the body.
Excessive vasoconstriction leads to high blood pressure.
Excessive vasodilation, as in shock, leads to low blood pressure.
Vascular smooth muscle cells play important roles during development.
Vascular smooth muscle cells drive osteocyte differentiation from undifferentiated precursors during osteogenesis.
Arteries have a great deal more smooth muscle within their walls than veins.
Arteries have greater wall thickness than veins.
Arteries carry pumped blood away from the heart to all organs and tissues.
Organs and tissues need oxygenated blood.
The endothelial lining of arteries and veins is similar.
Excessive proliferation of vascular smooth muscle cells contributes to the progression of pathological conditions.
Pathological conditions include vascular inflammation.
Pathological conditions include plaque formation ("Sclerosis (medicine)").
Pathological conditions include atherosclerosis.
Pathological conditions include restenosis.
Pathological conditions include pulmonary hypertension.
The majority of cells within atherosclerotic plaque are derived from vascular smooth muscle cells.
Atherosclerotic plaque is the predominant cause of heart attack and stroke.

