From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Series of books published from 1919 through 1978 by the Haldeman-Julius Publishing Company

|     |     |
| --- | --- |
| ! | This article includes a list of references, related reading, or external 

**********Extracted claims**********

Little Blue Books are a series of small staple-bound books.
Little Blue Books were published from 1919 through 1978.
The Haldeman-Julius Publishing Company of Girard, Kansas, published Little Blue Books.
Little Blue Books were extremely popular.
A total of 300 to 500 million booklets were sold over the series' lifetime.
A Big Blue Book range was also published.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius and his wife, Marcet, aimed to publish low-priced paperback pocketbooks.
These pocketbooks were intended to appeal to both the working class and the educated class.
The goal of the publishers was to disseminate works of literature, a wide range of ideas, common sense knowledge, and various points of view to a large audience.
The books, approximately 3½ by 5 inches (8.9 by 12.7 cm), easily fit into a working man's back pocket or shirt pocket.
The series was inspired by cheap 10-cent paperback editions of expired copyright classic works.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius had purchased these inspiring paperback editions as a 15-year-old.
In 1919, the Haldeman-Juliuses purchased a publishing house in Girard, Kansas.
The publishing house was purchased from their employer, Appeal to Reason.
Appeal to Reason was a socialist weekly that Emanuel Haldeman-Julius edited.
Appeal to Reason's printing presses were crucial to the Little Blue Books project.
Appeal to Reason's subscriber lists, totaling 175,000 names, were crucial to the Little Blue Books project.
Before printing began, Emanuel Haldeman-Julius asked Appeal to Reason's subscribers for an advance of $5.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius promised to send subscribers 50 pamphlets at staggered intervals using the upfront money.
Five thousand readers accepted Emanuel Haldeman-Julius's offer.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius had $25,000 to work with from the advance subscriptions.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius believed the initial 50 titles were good quality.
The booklets also sold well to readers who had not subscribed for batches of 50.
In 1919, the works began to be printed at a rate of 24,000 a day.
The initial series was called Appeal's Pocket Series.
The books were printed on cheap pulp paper.
The books were stapled.
The books were bound with a red stiff paper cover.
The initial price was 25 cents.
The series name changed over the first few years.
The color of the binding also changed over the first few years.
Previous names for the series included People's Pocket Series.
Previous names for the series included Appeal Pocket Series.
Previous names for the series included Ten Cent Pocket Series.
Previous names for the series included Five Cent Pocket Series.
The series was finally named Little Blue Books in 1923.
The price remained at 5 cents a copy for many years.
The idea of Little Blue Books caught on globally in nine years.
Little Blue Books were carried by laborers, scholars, and average citizens.
The St. Louis Dispatch referred to Haldeman-Julius as "the Henry Ford of literature."
Emperor Haile Selassie of Ethiopia supported the Little Blue Books.
W. E. B. Du Bois supported the Little Blue Books.
Admiral Richard Byrd supported the Little Blue Books.
Franklin P. Adams of Information, Please! supported the Little Blue Books.
Admiral Richard Byrd took a set of Little Blue Books to the South Pole.
Most Little Blue Books were sold by mail order.
Little Blue Books were promoted through sensationalistic advertisements.
Advertisements for Little Blue Books appeared in newspapers and magazines such as Life, Popular Science, and Ladies’ Home Journal.
To save advertisement space, only book titles were listed.
Book titles were organized by topic headings.
Topic headings included "Philosophy," "How-To," or "Sex."
Many classic works were shortened to meet publishing requirements.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius justified shortening classics by calling removed text "boring text."
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius pioneered the concept of cutting down classics, a concept later used by Reader's Digest.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius was a pioneer in guerrilla marketing.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius sold his books in bookstores, drugstores, toy stores, and through his own line of vending machines.
Mail-order customers checked off the titles they wanted and mailed in the order form.
A minimum order for mail-order customers was $1 for 20 books.
Many bookstores kept a book rack stocked with numerous Little Blue Book titles.
The small size and low price made Little Blue Books especially popular with travelers.
The small size and low price made Little Blue Books especially popular with transient working people.
If a book sold less than 10,000 copies in one year, Emanuel Haldeman-Julius would remove it from his line.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius usually attempted a new title for a slow-selling book before removal, often creating a hit.
"The Tallow Ball" by Guy de Maupassant sold 15,000 copies in one year.
"The Tallow Ball" sold 54,700 copies the next year after its title was changed to "A French Prostitute's Sacrifice."
Many famous people grew up reading Little Blue Books.
Louis L'Amour cites Little Blue Books as a major source of his early reading in his autobiography, *Education of a Wandering Man*.
Saul Bellow read Little Blue Books in his youth.
Harlan Ellison read Little Blue Books in his youth.
Jack Conroy read Little Blue Books in his youth.
Ralph Ellison read Little Blue Books in his youth.
William S. Burroughs read Little Blue Books in his youth.
Studs Terkel read Little Blue Books in his youth.
The works covered by Little Blue Books were frequently classics of Western literature.
Works by Goethe were well represented in Little Blue Books.
Works by Shakespeare were well represented in Little Blue Books.
Works by the Ancient Greeks were represented in Little Blue Books.
More modern writers like Voltaire were represented in Little Blue Books.
More modern writers like Émile Zola were represented in Little Blue Books.
More modern writers like H. G. Wells were represented in Little Blue Books.
Some topics covered by Little Blue Books were considered cutting edge for societal norms.
Little Blue Books included titles on making candy, such as #518 "How to Make All Kinds of Candy" by Helene Paquin.
Little Blue Books included classic literature titles, such as #246 "Hamlet" by William Shakespeare.
Little Blue Books explored topics like same-sex love, such as #692 "Homo-Sexual Life" by William J Fielding.
Agnostic viewpoints were explored in Little Blue Books, such as in #1500 "Why I Am an Agnostic: Including Expressions of Faith from a Protestant a Catholic and a Jew" by Clarence Darrow.
Shorter works from popular authors like Jack London were published.
Shorter works from popular authors like Henry David Thoreau were published.
A number of anti-religious tracts were published in the series.
Anti-religious tracts were written by Robert Ingersoll.
Anti-religious tracts were written by ex-Catholic priest Joseph McCabe.
Anti-religious tracts were written by Emanuel Haldeman-Julius himself.
A young Will Durant wrote a series of Blue Books on philosophy.
Will Durant's Blue Books on philosophy were republished in 1926 by Simon & Schuster as *The Story of Philosophy*.
*The Story of Philosophy* remains in print today.
Demand for existing Little Blue Book titles remained steady throughout the Depression.
Approximately 300 new Little Blue Book titles were released during the 1930s.
The majority of new titles released during the 1930s appeared prior to 1932.
Following World War II, the FBI under J. Edgar Hoover considered the Little Blue Books' inclusion of socialism as a threat.
Following World War II, the FBI under J. Edgar Hoover considered the Little Blue Books' inclusion of atheism as a threat.
Following World War II, the FBI under J. Edgar Hoover considered the Little Blue Books' frank treatment of sexuality as a threat.
The FBI placed Emanuel Haldeman-Julius on their enemies list.
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius was convicted of income tax evasion.
The persecution of Emanuel Haldeman-Julius caused a rapid decline in the number of bookstores carrying Little Blue Books.
Little Blue Books gradually faded into obscurity by the 1950s.
Older people who had read Little Blue Books in the 1920s and 1930s still remembered them.
The Cardinal Francis Spellman FBI file indicates the FBI's interest in Haldeman-Julius Publications by 1955.
An anonymous letter in late 1954 alerted the government to a book under press that was "vilifying" Cardinal Spellman.
At the time of Emanuel Haldeman-Julius's death on July 31, 1951, the series included 1873 active titles.
The works continued to be reprinted until the Girard printing plant and warehouse was destroyed by fire in 1978.
A total of 1914 titles were published in the series.
In the 1950s, The Truth Seeker, an atheist-Freethinker publication based in San Diego, California, purchased most of the remaining supply of Little Blue Books.
The Truth Seeker subsequently raised the prices of the books.
Collections of the Little Blue Book series are housed at Pittsburg State University.
Collections of the Little Blue Book series are housed at Kent State University.
Collections of the Little Blue Book series are housed at Bowling Green State University.
Collections of the Little Blue Book series are housed at California State University, Northridge.

