Title: Adela of Normandy
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adela_of_Normandy
Content: Jump to content

From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Countess of Blois from 1089 to 1102

For other uses, see Adela (disambiguation) "Adela (disambiguation)").

Not to be confused with Adelaide of Normandy, sister of William I of England.

| Adela of N

**********Extracted claims**********

Adela of Normandy served as Countess of Blois from 1089 to 1102.
Adela of Normandy was born around 1067 in the Duchy of Normandy.
Adela of Normandy died on March 8, 1137, at the age of 69–70 in Marcigny-sur-Loire, Kingdom of France.
Adela of Normandy was married to Stephen II, Count of Blois.
Adela of Normandy was a daughter of William the Conqueror.
Adela of Normandy's mother was Matilda of Flanders.
Adela of Normandy belonged to the House of Normandy.
Adela of Normandy practiced Roman Catholicism.
Adela of Normandy is also known as Saint Adela in the Catholic Church.
Adela of Normandy became the countess of Blois, Chartres, and Meaux through her marriage to Stephen II of Blois.
Stephen II of Blois greatly benefited from the increased social status and prestige associated with marrying into Adela of Normandy's wealthy and powerful family.
Adela of Normandy served as regent of Blois during her spouse's absence from 1096 to 1100 and from 1101 to 1102.
Adela of Normandy served as regent during her son's minority from 1102 until 1120.
Adela of Normandy's marriage laid the groundwork for a period of extended conflict in the Anglo-Norman lands.
Adela of Normandy was the mother of King Stephen of England.
King Stephen of England's taking of the throne over his niece Empress Matilda initiated the civil war known as The Anarchy.
Adela of Normandy is generally believed to have been born between 1066 and 1070.
Adela of Normandy was the youngest daughter of William the Conqueror.
Adela of Normandy was the favorite sister of King Henry I of England.
Adela of Normandy and King Henry I of England were likely the youngest children of William the Conqueror.
Adela of Normandy was a high-spirited and educated woman.
Adela of Normandy possessed a knowledge of Latin.
Adela of Normandy likely spent significant time and was educated at a monastery, probably the Abbey of Sainte-Trinité, Caen.
Adela of Normandy had three older brothers and one younger brother.
Adela of Normandy's bloodline was a valuable asset for marriage alliances.
Adela of Normandy married Stephen Henry, son and heir to the count of Blois, between 1080 and 1083.
Adela of Normandy was approximately fifteen years old when she married.
The marriage of Adela of Normandy and Stephen Henry took place at Chartres Cathedral.
The marriage created a strong familial alliance, linking the two most powerful families in Northern France.
Stephen Henry was nearly twenty years Adela of Normandy's senior.
Adela of Normandy and Stephen Henry had six or eight children who survived infancy during their fifteen-year marriage.
Stephen Henry inherited Blois, Chartres, and Meaux upon his father's death in 1089.
Stephen Henry also inherited lands and rights in parts of Berry and Burgundy.
The Thibaudian dynasty had other possessions east of Paris.
The Thibaudian dynasty's eastern possessions were coalescing into the county of Champagne by the end of Adela of Normandy's life.
Adela of Normandy helped establish the foundations for the emergence of the principality of Champagne.
Adela of Normandy and her husband had a relationship based on trust and mutual respect.
Adela of Normandy and her husband made decisions together.
Adela of Normandy swore to bind herself and her husband to protect the bishop of Chartres during a dispute with the king of France.
Stephen-Henry joined the First Crusade in 1096.
Adela of Normandy acted as regent for her husband during his extended absence as a leader of the First Crusade (1095–1098).
Adela of Normandy also acted as regent during her husband's second expedition in 1101.
Stephen's letters to Adela of Normandy offer insight into the experiences of the Crusade's leaders.
Stephen trusted Adela of Normandy to rule as regent while he was on crusade.
Adela of Normandy's regency included granting charters, such as the right to build new churches for monks.
Adela of Normandy collaborated with Ivo of Chartres at various times.
Adela of Normandy exchanged letters with Ivo of Chartres throughout her regency to discuss matters such as the control of misbehaving nuns and disputes about sworn oaths.
While regent, Adela of Normandy toured her lands.
While regent, Adela of Normandy settled disputes.
While regent, Adela of Normandy promoted economic growth.
While regent, Adela of Normandy commanded knights to go to battle with the king.
It was common for noble women to assume the duties of their male counterparts during the crusades.
The Count of Blois returned to France in 1100.
The Count of Blois brought several cartloads of maps, jewels, and other treasures, which he deposited at Chartres.
According to Orderic Vitalis, Adela of Normandy constantly criticized Stephen-Henry, even during their intimate moments, urging him to return to the Holy Land after he abandoned the First Crusade.
Stephen-Henry was under an obligation to the pope due to earlier agreements.
Stephen-Henry returned to Antioch to participate in the crusade of 1101.
Stephen-Henry was killed in a final stand after the Battle of Ramla in 1102.
Adela of Normandy continued to act as regent after her husband's death.
Adela of Normandy continued to act as regent through her son Thibaud's early rule until her retirement in 1120.
Even after Thibaud came of age, Adela of Normandy continued to issue charters.
Adela of Normandy acted as co-ruler of many parts of their land even after Thibaud came of age.
Adela of Normandy did not secure a marriage alliance for Thibaud.
Thibaud did not marry until after Adela of Normandy's retirement.
Adela of Normandy's decision not to secure a marriage alliance for Thibaud helped maintain her power and influence.
Adela of Normandy was a devout Benedictine sympathizer.
Adela of Normandy employed several high-ranking tutors to educate her children.
Adela of Normandy's youngest son, Henry, was conceived during the single year Stephen spent in France between crusading duties.
At two years of age, Henry was pledged to the Church at Cluny Abbey, Saône-et-Loire, France, as an oblate child.
Henry was dedicated to the service of God according to medieval practice.
Henry was later appointed Abbot of Glastonbury.
Henry was later appointed Bishop of Winchester.
As Bishop, Henry sponsored hundreds of constructions, including bridges, canals, palaces, forts, castles, and entire villages.
Bishop Henry built dozens of abbeys and chapels.
Bishop Henry sponsored books, including the Winchester Bible.
In 1105, Adela of Normandy was responsible for conveying the archbishop's serious threat of excommunication to her brother Henry I after St. Anselm visited her during a sickness.
Orderic Vitalis praises Adela of Normandy as a "wise and spirited woman" who capably governed her husband's estates and her own.
Adela of Normandy's power and interests are demonstrated in collected letters.
The collected letters show Adela of Normandy's religiosity and intellect.
In a letter from 1104, Adela of Normandy granted a monastery a portion of land with all its wildlife.
Adela of Normandy reserved the power to pass judgment on crimes committed in the granted area.
Adela of Normandy quarreled with her eldest son William.
Adela of Normandy appointed her younger brother Theobald to replace William as heir in 1107, despite William previously being named heir-designate.
Another son, Stephen of Blois, moved to London in 1111 to join the court of his uncle, King Henry I (Beauclerc).
Stephen of Blois became King Henry I (Beauclerc)'s favorite.
Upon Beauclerc's death in Normandy in 1135, Stephen seized the English throne from Holy Roman Empress Dowager Matilda.
Holy Roman Empress Dowager Matilda was King Henry I's daughter, whom he had named as his successor.
Stephen's seizure of the throne initiated a protracted civil war in England.
The civil war in England lasted nearly twenty years.
Adela of Normandy retired to the monastery at Marcigny in 1120.
Adela of Normandy became a nun upon her retirement.
Adela of Normandy chose the larger, more prestigious monastery at Marcigny, located near her son Henry at Cluny Abbey.
Adela of Normandy may have served as abbess of the community at Marcigny.
Adela of Normandy continued to interact and communicate with her children after retirement.
Adela of Normandy continued to interact and communicate with the ecclesiastical leaders of lands she had once ruled.
Adela of Normandy maintained her influence over the region after retirement.
Adela of Normandy sent letters to her son Thibaud and Geoffrey, bishop of Chartres, reminding them of her settlement of a monastic case.
Adela of Normandy's letters reminded her son Thibaud how his father and she regarded alms gifting to monasteries.
Later in 1120, Adela of Normandy's daughter Lucia-Mahaut drowned in the wreck of the White Ship alongside her husband.
Adela of Normandy lived long enough to witness her son Stephen on the English throne.
Adela of Normandy likely took pride in the ascension of her youngest child, Henry of Blois, to the bishopric of Winchester in 1129.
After Adela of Normandy's death in 1137 at Marcigny, prayers were offered at various churches she had personally endowed or recognized.
Adela and Stephen had several children.
William, Count of Sully, was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Theobald II, Count of Champagne, was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Odo of Blois was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Adela, who married Milo II of Montlhéry, was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Stephen, King of England, was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Lucia-Mahaut, who married Richard d'Avranches, 2nd Earl of Chester, was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Lucia-Mahaut and Richard d'Avranches both drowned on November 25, 1120, in the White Ship disaster.
Agnes, who married Hugh III of Le Puiset, was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Agnes and Hugh III of Le Puiset were the parents of Hugh de Puiset, Bishop of Durham.
Alix, who married Renaud III of Joigny, was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Henry, Bishop of Winchester (1096–1171), was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Henry, Bishop of Winchester, was an oblate child raised at Cluny Abbey in Saône-et-Loire, France.
Eleanor of Champagne was a child of Adela and Stephen.
Some of the listed daughters may have been step-daughters of Adela.
Adela had five biological sons.
Adela may have had three or more daughters.
Not all of the listed daughters were necessarily Adela's biological children.
Adela's daughters are not mentioned by name during their youth.
Adela's daughters are mentioned in records only when they reached marriageable age.
Adela's daughters played an important role in building alliances.
Adela is a saint in the Roman Catholic Church.
Adela's feast day is February 24.
Kimberly A. LoPrete states that Milo bigamously married an unnamed daughter of Adela.
The marriage between Milo and Adela's unnamed daughter was annulled.

