**********Extracted claims**********

Umberto Blasi was born on October 12, 1886.
Umberto Blasi died on July 1, 1938.
Umberto Blasi was an Italian long-distance runner.
Umberto Blasi was a three-time national champion in the marathon.
Umberto Blasi competed in the men's marathon at the 1908 Summer Olympics.
Umberto Blasi won Italy's first national marathon championship on June 3, 1908.
Umberto Blasi's winning time for Italy's first national marathon championship was 3:01:04.
Italy's first national marathon championship was held in Rome.
Umberto Blasi earned a spot on the Italian Olympic team for the 1908 Games.
Augusto Cocca and Dorando Pietri also earned spots on the Italian Olympic team for the 1908 Games.
56 athletes started the marathon in London in July 1908.
Umberto Blasi dropped out of the 1908 Olympic marathon after 8 miles.
Umberto Blasi did not finish the 1908 Olympic marathon.
Umberto Blasi won his second consecutive national championship on September 19, 1909.
Umberto Blasi's winning time for his second national championship was 2:38:30.
Umberto Blasi's second national championship was run on a 40-kilometer course in Milan.
Umberto Blasi achieved a mark of 2:48:44 on an official distance course in Voltri, Italy.
Umberto Blasi's 2:48:44 mark lowered the Italian marathon record.
Arturo deMaria had set the previous Italian marathon record three months prior to Umberto Blasi's mark in Voltri.
Umberto Blasi was one of ten professional competitors in a marathon held on April 2, 1910.
The marathon on April 2, 1910, was held at the Polo Grounds in New York City.
16,000 spectators attended the marathon at the Polo Grounds on April 2, 1910.
Umberto Blasi dropped out of the April 2, 1910 marathon after 18 miles.
Supporters of Umberto Blasi and Fortunio Zantis caused an early end to a marathon in Rocky Point, Rhode Island, on July 10, 1910.
The supporters crowded the track and attempted to trip Patrick Dineen of Boston, who was the leader in the July 10, 1910 marathon.
Patrick Dineen was declared the winner of the July 10, 1910 marathon.
Patrick Dineen completed 21 laps in the July 10, 1910 marathon.
Umberto Blasi finished in fifth place in the July 10, 1910 marathon.
Umberto Blasi competed with five other runners in a race at the Rocky Point baseball grounds.
3,500 spectators witnessed the race at the Rocky Point baseball grounds.
The race at the Rocky Point baseball grounds was a 20-mile race.
Umberto Blasi finished in fourth place in the race at the Rocky Point baseball grounds.
Umberto Blasi ran his marathon personal best with a 2:38:00.8 performance.
Umberto Blasi captured his third and final national title with a time of 2:38:00.8.
Umberto Blasi's third national title race took place in Legnano, Italy, on November 29, 1914.
The International Association of Athletics Federations does not recognize Umberto Blasi's 2:38:00.8 mark as a world best.
The Association of Road Racing Statisticians lists Umberto Blasi's 2:38:00.8 mark in their progression of world records in the marathon.
The Association of Road Racing Statisticians lists Umberto Blasi's 2:38:00.8 mark as the world's fastest for 1914.
Umberto Blasi was born in Rome, Italy.

