From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Israeli activist; founder of United Hatzalah

| Eli Beer |
| --- |
| ! |
| Born | (1973-09-13) September 13, 1973 (age 51) |
| Nationality | Israeli |
| Known for | Founder of United Hatzalah |

**El

**********Extracted claims**********

Eli Beer was born on September 13, 1973, in Israel.
Eli Beer is the founder of United Hatzalah of Israel.
Eli Beer is the President of the U.S.-based organization Friends of United Hatzalah.
United Hatzalah of Israel is an independent, non-profit, fully-volunteer emergency medical services organization.
United Hatzalah of Israel provides fast emergency medical first response throughout Israel.
United Hatzalah of Israel provides free emergency medical first response throughout Israel.
Eli Beer witnessed the bombing of the #12 bus on June 2, 1978.
Eli Beer was inspired by the 1978 bus bombing to volunteer on an ambulance.
Eli Beer volunteered on an ambulance at the age of 15.
Eli Beer attended school and worked in his family's bookstore and real estate business while volunteering.
Eli Beer was seriously ill with Covid-19.
Eli Beer was placed in a coma twice due to Covid-19.
Eli Beer began working with Hatzalah Jerusalem in 1992.
Eli Beer served as a fundraiser and operational coordinator for Hatzalah Jerusalem.
Hatzalah Jerusalem grew and changed its name to Hatzalah Israel.
Hatzalah Israel reflected a nationwide scope.
Hatzalah Israel functioned as an umbrella organization.
Hatzalah Israel incorporated many Hatzalah chapters from across the country.
A terror attack occurred in the Beit Yisroel neighborhood of Jerusalem in 2002.
First response was slowed by narrow roadways and congestion in the Beit Yisroel neighborhood of Jerusalem in 2002.
A volunteer from the organization conceived the idea of first responders arriving with medical equipment on motorcycles.
The 'ambucycle' concept was created.
The term 'ambucycle' was coined by the organization.
An ambucycle is an ambulance-motorcycle.
Ambucycles carry a full complement of medical equipment, similar to a regular ambulance.
Ambucycles do not carry a backboard, stair-chair, or bed.
The Second Lebanon War occurred in 2006.
Eli Beer unified numerous smaller Hatzalah organizations after the Second Lebanon War.
The organization's name was changed to United Hatzalah.
United Hatzalah is divided into branches.
United Hatzalah includes Jewish, Muslim, Druze, and Christian volunteers from all religious spectrums.
United Hatzalah established its reputation as an EMS organization.
United Hatzalah has been at the forefront of medical innovation.
United Hatzalah was the first EMS to introduce the Ambucycle.
Ambucycles allow volunteer first responders to reach patients in an average of 3 minutes across Israel.
Ambucycle response times are typically 8 to 15 minutes faster than the first ambulance.
United Hatzalah launched Israel's first GPS-based dispatch system in 2008.
The GPS-based dispatch system locates and dispatches the five closest EMS responders within 3 seconds of an emergency.
United Hatzalah has grown to include more than 8,000 volunteer medical first responders.
United Hatzalah's volunteer medical first responders include EMTs, paramedics, and doctors.
United Hatzalah volunteers responded to more than 700,000 medical emergencies in 2024.
United Hatzalah's services are provided free of charge.
United Hatzalah's services are provided to all people regardless of race, nationality, or religion.
Eli Beer expanded United Hatzalah internationally in 2015.
Branches of United Hatzalah were established in South America and other countries.
"United Rescue" was established in Jersey City, N.J., in the United States.
The response time in Jersey City, N.J. was reduced to two minutes and thirty-five seconds as a result of United Rescue.
Eli Beer has been a medical responder at the scenes of the Ben Yehuda Street bombings.
Eli Beer has been a medical responder at the Versailles Wedding Hall disaster.
Eli Beer has been a medical responder during the Second Lebanon War in the north.
Eli Beer has been a medical responder during Operation Cast Lead in the south.
Eli Beer has been a medical responder at dozens of other major terror attacks and mass casualty incidents.
Eli Beer played a pivotal role in providing critical humanitarian assistance and medical care during the Gaza war.
Eli Beer helped set up a United Hatzalah field hospital and dispatch.
Eli Beer helped send over 1,500 volunteer EMTs to the Gaza periphery.
Volunteer EMTs treated civilian and military personnel wounded in the Hamas terror attacks in the Gaza periphery.
Eli Beer helped mobilize tens of thousands of donors worldwide to raise funds for critical medical supplies.
Medical supplies were depleted due to United Hatzalah's humanitarian response to wounded people in Israel.
Eli Beer met with U.S. President Joe Biden during President Biden's visit to Israel.
Eli Beer disseminated a story about an Israeli baby being placed in an oven and burned to death at a Republican Jewish Coalition’s Annual Leadership Summit on October 28, 2023.
The story disseminated by Eli Beer about an Israeli baby being burned to death was unable to be verified.
Eli Beer was featured in interviews discussing United Hatzalah volunteers' response on October 7.
These interviews included appearances on Fox News with Jake Tapper, CNN, and CBS News.
Eli Beer received the Social Entrepreneur Award from the Schwab Foundation for Social Entrepreneurship in cooperation with the World Economic Forum of Davos in 2010.
Eli Beer received the Presidential Award for Volunteerism from Shimon Peres in 2011.
Eli Beer was chosen as one of the World Economic Forum's Young Global Leaders in 2012.
Eli Beer was nominated for the Young Global Leader award by a committee chaired by Queen Rania of Jordan.
Eli Beer was recognized for his efforts to create a multicultural, apolitical medical rescue organization.
Eli Beer received the Victor J. Goldberg IIE Prize for Peace in the Middle East in 2013.
Eli Beer received the Victor J. Goldberg IIE Prize jointly with Murad Alian.
Murad Alian is the Coordinator of United Hatzalah's Team in Israeli-occupied East Jerusalem.
Eli Beer received the Champion of Human Life Award in 2016.
The Champion of Human Life Award was presented at the Fourth Annual Champions of Jewish Values International Awards Gala.
The World Values Network sponsored the Champions of Jewish Values International Awards Gala.
Eli Beer was awarded the CER Internet Entrepreneur Prize in Helsinki in 2017.
Eli Beer received the CER Internet Entrepreneur Prize for developing United Hatzalah's LifeCompass app.
The LifeCompass app automatically dispatches the five closest EMT responders within three seconds of an emergency.
Eli Beer was invited to speak on TEDMED in April 2013.
Eli Beer's TEDMED talk was featured on the TED website.
Eli Beer spoke at the TEDx Gateway conference in India in February 2020.
Eli Beer was selected as one of the Jerusalem Post's 50 most influential Jews in October 2024.
The selection was based on United Hatzalah's lifesaving response to October 7th.
The selection was based on United Hatzalah's innovations, including the ambucycle and GPS-based dispatch.
United Hatzalah was awarded the 2024 Health Minister’s Shield for Volunteering.
The Health Minister’s Shield recognized United Hatzalah's lifesaving efforts during the October 7th attacks.
The Health Minister’s Shield recognized United Hatzalah's lifesaving efforts during the Iron Swords War.
The Health Minister’s Shield recognized United Hatzalah's ongoing contribution to public health in Israel.

