From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Soccer stadium in Korat, Thailand

|     |     |
| --- | --- |
| ! | This article includes a list of general

**********Extracted claims**********

The 80th Birthday Stadium is a soccer stadium located in Korat, Thailand.
The full name of the stadium is His Majesty the King's 80th Birthday Anniversary, 5th December 2007 Stadium.
The stadium was formerly known as 333rd Nakhon Ratchasima Anniversary Stadium.
The stadium is located in Mueang Nakhon Ratchasima, Thailand.
The stadium's coordinates are 14°55′38″N 102°02′56″E.
The stadium has a capacity of 25,000 spectators.
The playing surface of the stadium is grass.
Construction of the stadium broke ground on March 28, 2005.
The stadium was built between March 2005 and July 2007.
The stadium officially opened in December 2007.
The construction cost of the entire SEA Games complex, which includes the stadium, was US$65 million.
Nakhon Ratchasima F.C. has been a tenant of the stadium since 2009.
The 80th Birthday Stadium is the main stadium in His Majesty the King's 80th Birthday Anniversary, 5 December 2007, Sports Complex.
Nakhon Ratchasima FC, a professional team, uses the stadium as its home.
The stadium was used for the 2007 Southeast Asian Games.
The 2007 Southeast Asian Games coincided with the 80th birthday of King Bhumibol Adulyadej.
The venue's name, 80th Birthday Stadium, reflects the 80th birthday of King Bhumibol Adulyadej.
The stadium is located in the former SEA Games sports complex on Highway 304 (Pak Thong Chai Road).
The stadium is located southwest of the city of Nakhon Ratchasima.
The stadium provides all-seated accommodation.
The stadium has space for 24,641 spectators.
The stadium's tribunes form a continuous ring around the pitch and running track.
The stands of the stadium are uncovered on three sides.
A large cantilevered roof provides cover for approximately 7,000 seats on one side of the stadium.
The stadium's ring rises to approximately double the height of the tribune it faces on one side.
Most of the seats in the stadium are bright orange.
The bright orange color of the stadium seats matches Nakhon Ratchasima FC's home kit.
The stadium seats are of the fixed-bucket style.
Some seats at the top of the main stand tribune are red.
The seats in the VIP section of the main stand are of the tip-up type.
The initials "SAT" (Sports Authority of Thailand) are depicted in blue in the lower sections of the main stand.
Equivalent letters in Thai script are also depicted in blue in the lower sections of the main stand.
Commentary boxes and private suites are located at the top of the main stand.
A large royal box is located in the middle of the main stand.
A large scoreboard is located at the north end of the stadium.
Three flagpoles used for the SEA Games are located at the end opposite the scoreboard.
The place where the "Olympic" flame burned is located at the end opposite the scoreboard.
The stadium's concourses are basic.
The concourses extend around the entire stadium.
The stadium's entrances lead directly into the seating areas.
The stadium has modern and well-appointed toilets for supporters.
The stadium pitch is known to drain well in the rainy season.
Two floodlight systems are in use at the stadium.
Roof-mounted lights are installed on the main stand side.
Four very tall concrete pylons serving as floodlights stand outside the stadium.
Both floodlight systems were installed to allow simultaneous illumination of the running track, pitch, sand pits, high-jump, pole vault, and shot-put areas during the Southeast Asian Games.
A floodlit pitch, complete with a running track and a small stand, is located opposite the main entrance to the stadium.
This secondary ground does not meet league standards.
This secondary ground is not enclosed.
The floodlights of the secondary ground are not powerful enough for evening matches.
Nakhon Ratchasima FC used this secondary ground for a pre-season friendly in March 2009 against Khonkaen FC.
Nakhon Ratchasima FC moved into the stadium in June 2008.
Nakhon Ratchasima FC previously played at the Nakhon Ratchasima Municipal Stadium.
The club's move to the stadium was initially on a trial basis.
The club's switch to the stadium has been successful.
Nakhon Ratchasima FC continues to use the stadium for home matches.
The Yemen national football team used the stadium for its home matches for the 2027 AFC Asian Cup qualification in 2025.
The stadium hosted a 2010 King's Cup match between Thailand and Singapore on January 17, 2010, which Thailand won 1–0.
The stadium hosted a 2010 King's Cup match between Poland and Thailand on January 20, 2010, which Poland won 3–1.
The stadium hosted a 2010 King's Cup match between Poland and Singapore on January 23, 2010, which Poland won 6–1.
The stadium hosted a friendly match between Thailand and Philippines on November 9, 2014, which Thailand won 3–0.
The stadium hosted a friendly match between Thailand and New Zealand on November 18, 2014, which Thailand won 2–0.
Yemen is scheduled to play Lebanon at the stadium on March 25, 2025, as part of the 2027 AFC Asian Cup qualification.
Yemen is scheduled to play Brunei at the stadium on October 14, 2025.
Yemen is scheduled to play Bhutan at the stadium on November 18, 2025.

