Title: IEEE Rebooting Computing
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_Rebooting_Computing
Content: Jump to content

From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia

Initiative to rethink the concept of computing

|     |     |
| --- | --- |
| ! | **A major contributor to this article appears to have a close connection with its subject.** It may require cleanup t

**********Extracted claims**********

The Task Force on Rebooting Computing (TFRC) is housed within the IEEE Computer Society.
The Task Force on Rebooting Computing (TFRC) is the new home for the IEEE Rebooting Computing Initiative.
The IEEE Rebooting Computing Initiative was founded in 2013 by the IEEE Future Directions Committee.
Rebooting Computing has provided an international and interdisciplinary environment.
This environment allows experts from a wide variety of computer-related fields to explore novel approaches to future computing.
IEEE Rebooting Computing began as a global initiative launched by IEEE.
IEEE Rebooting Computing proposes to rethink the concept of computing through a holistic look at all aspects of computing.
The initiative considers aspects ranging from the computing device itself to the user interface.
IEEE Rebooting Computing provides access to various resources, including conferences, educational events, feature articles, scholarly articles, reports, and videos.
The IEEE Future Directions Committee established an "IEEE Rebooting Computing" working group in late 2012.
The broad vision of the working group is to "reboot" the entire field of computer technology.
The activities of the working group are carried out by the IEEE Rebooting Computing Committee.
The IEEE Rebooting Computing Committee is a team of volunteers from ten participating IEEE Societies and Councils.
The term "rebooting computing" was coined by IEEE Life Fellow Peter Denning.
The term "rebooting computing" was part of an early U.S. National Science Foundation-sponsored project focused on revamping computer education.
IEEE Rebooting Computing hosted four invitation-only summits between 2013 and 2015.
These summits were held in Washington, D.C., and Santa Cruz, California.
The summits addressed the future of computing from a holistic point of view.
IEEE Rebooting Computing adopted its logo in 2014.
The logo consists of an exploding infinity symbol.
The logo is intended to suggest the absence of limits for future computing technology.
IEEE Rebooting Computing announced the signing of a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) with the International Technology Roadmap for Semiconductors (ITRS) in March 2015.
The MOU led to the formation of the IEEE International Roadmap for Devices and Systems (IRDS) in May 2016.
The IRDS incorporated the previous mission of the ITRS in semiconductor device fabrication.
The IRDS expanded its scope to encompass alternative technologies, computer architectures, and system applications.
IEEE Rebooting Computing announced support for the National Strategic Computing Initiative (NSCI) in September 2015.
The NSCI was established under Executive Order 13702, issued by U.S. President Barack Obama in July 2015.
The NSCI calls for a coordinated Federal strategy in high-performance computing (HPC) research, development, and deployment.
The National Nanotechnology Initiative (NNI) announced a "Nanotechnology-Inspired Grand Challenge in Future Computing" in October 2015.
A key document cited by the NNI as part of this grand challenge is a white paper entitled _Sensible Machines_.
_Sensible Machines_ was co-sponsored by IEEE Rebooting Computing and ITRS.
The IEEE New Initiatives Committee renewed the mandate of the Rebooting Computing Initiative in 2017.
The renewed mandate included five major activities: the International Conference on Rebooting Computing (ICRC), IRDS, the Industry Summit on the Future of Computing, the Low-Power Image Recognition Challenge (LPIRC), and a Workshop on the Confluence of Artificial Intelligence and Cybersecurity.
A new activity was added in 2018 to promote the development of quantum computing.
The IEEE Rebooting Computing Task Force aims to help return the computing industry to exponential computer-performance scaling.
Computer-performance scaling stalled in 2005.
The stall was due to energy inefficiencies of CMOS-based classical computers.
Historically, computer processing power doubled every 18 months due to increasing densities of transistors per semiconductor unit.
A move toward instruction-level parallelism and superscalar microprocessors occurred to alleviate challenges and sustain regular processing performance gains.
Rising costs associated with greater power consumption signaled the end of Moore's Law.
The IEEE introduced the IEEE Rebooting Computing initiative due to these rising costs and the signaling of Moore's Law's end.
The initiative incorporates three fundamental pillars of rebooting computing: energy efficiency, security, and Human Computer Interface (HCI).
The initiative seeks to overcome setbacks and challenges related to the deceleration of computational power and capacity.
These efforts may also be applied in other technology sectors, such as the Internet of Things.
IEEE Rebooting Computing encompasses a variety of activities, products, and services.
These efforts include an online web portal, a technical community, publications, conferences, and events.
IEEE Rebooting Computing maintains a collaborative partnership with IRDS.
The initiative responds to and participates in national and international initiatives, including the National Strategic Computing Initiative (NSCI) and the "Nanotechnology Inspired Grand Challenge for Future Computing".
The web portal is the primary online home for IEEE Rebooting Computing.
The website provides relevant news, information, and resources to users.
Resources include articles authored by IEEE experts and third-party publications.
The web portal includes access to a list of both IEEE-sponsored and general industry conferences and events, videos, and historical data from past summits.
The web portal also hosts the IEEE Rebooting Computing Podcast.
The podcast is a collection of interviews with leaders in the field.
The podcast is updated monthly.
The podcast collection is also hosted on the video website IEEE.tv.
IEEE Technical Communities are virtual communities for practitioners, subject matter experts, researchers, and other technology professionals.
The IEEE Computer Society Rebooting Computing Technical Community serves as a venue for the distribution and dissemination of news, announcements, and other information.
An email newsletter is distributed monthly to several thousand community members.
The newsletter includes free access to specially selected recent articles of interest from the IEEE Xplore library of journals and conference proceedings.
IEEE membership is not required to become a member of the IEEE Rebooting Computing Technical Community.
IEEE Rebooting Computing sponsors, co-sponsors, and participates in a variety of technology conferences and events worldwide.
Conference and event programming is designed to stimulate discussion of existing and emerging technologies, including challenges, benefits, and opportunities.
Conference and event programming generally encompasses keynote addresses, panel discussions, paper presentations, poster sessions, tutorials, and workshops.
During its first several years, the initiative's flagship event series was its Rebooting Computing Summits.
The inaugural IEEE Rebooting Computing Summit was held in December 2013 in Washington, D.C.
The event drew business, industry, government, and academic representatives from the U.S. and internationally.
A second IEEE Rebooting Computing Summit was held in May 2014 in Santa Cruz, California.
A group of invited business, trade, academia, and government experts discussed neuromorphic engineering, approximate computing, and adiabatic/reversible computing at the second summit.
IEEE Rebooting Computing held a third summit in October 2014 in Scotts Valley, California.
The theme for the third summit was "Rethinking Structures of Computation".
The third summit focused on the topics of parallel computing, security, approximation, and Human-Computer Interaction.
A fourth IEEE Rebooting Computing Summit (RCS4) was held in December 2015 in Washington D.C.
The theme for RCS4 was "Roadmapping the Future of Computing: Discovering How We May Compute".
The fourth summit included plenary talks and breakout groups in three tracks.
The tracks were "Probabilistic/Approximate Computing", "Neuromorphic Computing", and "Beyond CMOS/3D Computing", with a fourth track on "Superconducting Computing".
The fourth summit also hosted speakers from other programs promoting future computing.
The IEEE International Conference on Rebooting Computing (ICRC 2016) was held in October 2016 in San Diego, California.
The goal of ICRC 2016 was to discover and foster novel methodologies to reinvent computing technology.
This includes new materials and physics, devices and circuits, system and network architectures, and algorithms and software.
Proceedings of the ICRC 2016 have been published by IEEE.
Videos of many of the ICRC 2016 presentations are available online.
The second conference in this series, ICRC 2017, was held in November 2017 in Washington, DC.
A third conference in this series, ICRC 2018, was held in Washington, DC in November 2018.
IEEE Rebooting Computing sponsored a distinct one-day summit in November 2017.
This Industry Summit featured plenary presentations by industry, government, and academic leaders.
The summit featured a new public announcement from IBM Research on a breakthrough in quantum computing technology.
Other topics of interest included artificial intelligence, machine learning, memory-driven computing, and heterogeneous computing.
A second Industry Summit was held in 2018.
IEEE Rebooting Computing held the first-ever Low-Power Image Recognition Competition (LPIRC) in June 2015.
The LPIRC was held as a one-day workshop during the 2015 Design Automation Conference in San Francisco, California.
The competition aimed to assess the state of low-power approaches to object detection in images.
The competition fielded competitors from four different countries, including teams from Carnegie Mellon, Rice University, Tsinghua University, and Huawei.
Before the competition, training data was released for detection from the ImageNet Large-Scale Visual Recognition Challenge (ILSVRC).
Source code of the referee system was released to the public in March 2015.
An intranet was established for contestants to retrieve provided image files from and return answers to the competition's referee system.
Teams were given 10 minutes to process images.
Images were ranked by detection accuracy and energy usage.
A second LPIRC was held during the June 2016 Design Automation Conference in Austin, Texas.
A third LPIRC was held in July 2017 as part of the Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Conference (CVPR) in Honolulu, Hawaii.
Two LPIRC competitions were held in 2018.
One 2018 LPIRC was held at CVPR in Salt Lake City, Utah in June, and a second online competition was held in November.
These competitions included major new sponsors Google and Facebook.
An overview of the first three years of LPIRC was presented at the 2018 IEEE Conference on Design Automation and Test in Europe.
A three-day IEEE Confluence Event, the IEEE Workshop on Cybersecurity and Artificial Intelligence, was held in October 2017.
This workshop was co-chaired by Dr. Dejan Milojicic, a co-chair of Rebooting Computing.
The aim of the workshop was to develop a strategy to coordinate efforts to apply AI/ML to improve cybersecurity worldwide.
Following this workshop, an IEEE Trend Paper was published.
The paper was entitled "Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Applied to Cybersecurity".
The paper included recommendations for new standards and regulations for industry and government.
A second workshop was held in November 2018.
An invitation-only IEEE Quantum Computing Summit was held in August 2018 in Atlanta, Georgia.
This effort has been led by Dr. Erik DeBenedictis, one of the co-chairs of Rebooting Computing.
The summit led to a White Paper on the subject of quantum computing.
"Rebooting Computing Week" was created in 2017.
Rebooting Computing Week was created to have a common location for annual conferences and workshops associated with Rebooting Computing.
In 2017 and 2018, Rebooting Computing Week was held in November in the Washington, DC area.
Events in 2018 included ICRC, the Industry Summit, the IRDS Workshop, the Cybersecurity Workshop, and the Quantum Computing Workshop.
IEEE Rebooting Computing members and societies regularly publish papers, manuscripts, journals, magazines, and other documents.
IEEE Rebooting Computing contributes to or features articles from various IEEE publications.
These publications include _Computer_, _IEEE Journal on Emerging and Selected Topics in Circuits and Systems_, _IEEE Journal on Exploratory Solid-State Computational Devices and Circuits_, _IEEE Solid-State Circuits Magazine_, _IEEE Spectrum_, and _Proceedings of the IEEE_.
In December 2015, _Computer_ published a special issue on rebooting computing.
Members of the IEEE Rebooting Committee served as guest editors and contributors for the _Computer_ special issue.
In November 2016, the Italian online magazine _Mondo Digitale_ published an article.
The article was entitled "Rebooting Computing: Developing a Roadmap for the Future of the Computer Industry."
In March 2017, _Computing in Science and Engineering_ published a special issue on "The End of Moore's Law".
This issue addressed alternative approaches to maintaining exponential growth in performance.
Since 2016, _Computer_ has published a series of columns under the heading "Rebooting Computing".
The columns are coordinated by RC co-chair Dr. Erik DeBenedictis.
IEEE Rebooting Computing also contributes to a variety of trade publications and news outlets.
These outlets include _EE Times_ and _Scientific Computing_.
IEEE Rebooting Computing began as a multi-society participation from a cross-section of IEEE societies.
This participation comes from a cross-section of IEEE societies with interest in numerous aspects of computing.
These aspects include circuits and systems design, architectures, design automation, magnetics, nanotechnology, reliability, and superconductors.
Ten IEEE Societies and Councils participate in the IEEE Rebooting Computing initiative.
These participating entities are the IEEE Circuits and Systems Society, IEEE Components, Packaging, and Manufacturing Technology Society, IEEE Computer Society, IEEE Council on Electronic Design Automation, IEEE Technical Council on Superconductivity, IEEE Electron Devices Society, IEEE Magnetics Society, IEEE Nanotechnology Council, IEEE Reliability Society, and IEEE Solid-State Circuits Society.
IEEE Rebooting Computing has established a collaborative relationship with the ITRS.
This collaboration began with an exchange of information in 2014.
Following the signing of a formal collaboration agreement, IEEE Rebooting Computing and ITRS arranged and held joint international workshops in 2015.
The objective of these workshops was to identify computer performance scaling challenges and establish a roadmap to successfully restart computer performance scaling.
IEEE Rebooting Computing further collaborated with ITRS on a new effort known as ITRS 2.0.
ITRS 2.0 extends beyond traditional Moore's Law scaling of chips.
ITRS 2.0 includes roadmaps covering systems and applications.
ITRS Chairman Paolo Gargini has stated that ITRS shares IEEE Rebooting Computing's mission.
The shared mission is to restore computing to its historic exponential performance scaling trends.
The agreement between the two organizations will ensure they help fundamentally shift the computer industry's focus, resources, time, and attention onto new possibilities for computational performance.
On May 4, 2016, the IEEE announced the launch of the "International Roadmap for Devices and Systems" (IRDS).
IRDS operates as part of the IEEE Standards Association's (IEEE-SA) Industry Connections program.
IRDS is sponsored by IEEE Rebooting Computing in consultation with the IEEE Computer Society and ITRS.
IRDS will provide guidance on future trends in computer systems, architectures, software, chips, and other components across the entire computer industry.
IRDS is modeled on ITRS roadmaps.
ITRS roadmaps previously guided the semiconductor industry during the Moore's Law era.
The first IRDS Roadmap was released in the first quarter of 2018.
The IEEE Rebooting Computing initiative has begun to influence both the technology industry and national policy efforts.
The initiative is releasing the IRDS _Roadmap of Future Computing_.
The roadmap includes the development of performance benchmarks and standards for new classes of computer systems.
Addressing roadblocks in future high-performance computing, also known as exascale computing, is a key area of focus for IEEE Rebooting Computing.
The initiative has been actively pursuing and aiding the industry in making progress toward possible solutions.
Possible solutions include specialized chip architectures, millivolt switches, and 3D integrated circuits.
These solutions were noted by Dr. Erik DeBenedictis of Sandia National Laboratories in "Power Problems Threaten to Strangle Exascale Computing".
In February 2015, IEEE Rebooting Computing Senior Program Director Bichlien Hoang and co-author Sin-Kuen Hawkins received a "Best Presentation Award".
The award was for their paper, "How Will Rebooting Computing Help IoT".
The paper was presented at the 18th International Conference on Intelligence in Next Generation Networks (ICIN 2015) in Paris, France.
The paper described IEEE Rebooting Computing's approach to addressing technical challenges generated by IoT and other key computing trends.
One of the key features of the future computing environment is its heterogeneous nature.
This involves combining different types of processors.
In January 2018, the Office of Advanced Scientific Computing Research of the US Department of Energy held a Workshop on Extreme Heterogeneity.
The invited plenary talk of the workshop was on the IEEE Rebooting Computing Initiative.
This talk was presented by Professor Tom Conte of Georgia Tech, a co-chair of the Initiative.
Media coverage of IEEE Rebooting Computing's efforts has increased.
In May 2016, a New York Times feature article quoted IEEE Rebooting Co-Chair, Professor Thomas M. Conte.
The New York Times article was on the technological and economic implications of the ending of Moore's Law.
Other publications reporting on IEEE Rebooting Computing activities include _EE Times_, _HPCWire_, _IEEE Spectrum_, _Inside HPC_, _Scientific Computing_, _SiliconANGLE_, and _VR World_.
In November 2018, _Forbes Magazine_ published an article featuring IRDS.
The article was entitled "IEEE Roadmaps Guide Future Memories and Applications".
The 2017 ICRC was featured in an _IEEE Spectrum_ news article.
The _IEEE Spectrum_ article was entitled "Four Strange New Ways to Compute".
The IEEE Rebooting Computing organization was founded in December 2012.
The IEEE Rebooting Computing is a professional organization.
The focus of IEEE Rebooting Computing is computing.
The location of IEEE Rebooting Computing is Piscataway, New Jersey, U.S.
The IEEE Rebooting Computing originated as a global initiative launched by IEEE.
The IEEE Rebooting Computing serves a worldwide area.
The methods of IEEE Rebooting Computing include communications, conferences, digital media, education, industry standards, marketing, publications, and a web portal.

