| Operator | United Space Alliance |
| Builder | Atlantic Marine Shipyard, Fort George Island, Flori

**********Extracted claims**********

MV _Liberty Star_ was formerly owned by NASA.
MV _Liberty Star_ was operated by United Space Alliance.
MV _Liberty Star_ primarily served as a Solid Rocket Booster (SRB) recovery ship following Space Shuttle missions.
MV _Liberty Star_ also performed tugboat duties.
MV _Liberty Star_ also acted as a research platform.
The vessel was transferred to the U.S. Department of Transportation in 2012.
The vessel is currently used as a training vessel at the United States Merchant Marine Academy.
The vessel was renamed TV _Kings Pointer_.
MV _Freedom Star_ is the sister ship of MV _Liberty Star_.
MV _Liberty Star_ was built by Atlantic Marine Shipyard, located on Fort George Island, Florida, U.S.
MV _Liberty Star_ was christened on September 27, 1980.
MV _Liberty Star_ was acquired in October 1980.
MV _Liberty Star_ began service in January 1981.
MV _Liberty Star_ was taken out of service on September 14, 2012.
The homeport of MV _Liberty Star_ was Port Canaveral, Florida, U.S.
The call sign for MV _Liberty Star_ is WRPH.
The IMO number for MV _Liberty Star_ is 7925302.
The MMSI number for MV _Liberty Star_ is 338990000.
MV _Liberty Star_ was transferred to the Maritime Administration upon being taken out of service by NASA.
The vessel's post-transfer name is TV _Kings Pointer_.
The owner of TV _Kings Pointer_ is the U.S. Department of Transportation.
The operator of TV _Kings Pointer_ is the U.S. Maritime Administration.
TV _Kings Pointer_ was acquired on September 14, 2012.
The homeport of TV _Kings Pointer_ is Kings Point, New York, U.S.
TV _Kings Pointer_ is currently in service.
The tonnage of the vessel is 484 Gross Tonnage (GT) and 743 Gross Registered Tonnage (GRT).
The tonnage of the vessel is 239 Net Tonnage (NT) and 222 Net Registered Tonnage (NRT).
The displacement of the vessel is 1,052 short tons (954 metric tons).
The length of the vessel is 176 feet (53.6 meters).
The beam of the vessel is 37 feet (11.3 meters).
The height of the vessel is 72 feet (21.9 meters).
The draft of the vessel is 12 feet (3.7 meters).
The depth of the vessel is 15 feet (4.6 meters).
The installed power of the vessel includes two 223 horsepower (166 kW) Kato generators.
The propulsion of the vessel includes two 1,450 horsepower (1,080 kW) GM EMD 12-645E6A diesel engines.
The diesel engines drive two propellers.
The propulsion of the vessel also includes two 450 horsepower (340 kW) Detroit Diesel 8V71 engines.
The Detroit Diesel engines drive bow and stern thrusters.
The speed of the vessel is 15 knots (28 km/h; 17 mph).
The range of the vessel is 6,900 miles (11,100 km).
The endurance of the vessel is 30 days.
The maximum capacity of the vessel is 24 persons.
The vessel's complement includes 10 crew members.
The vessel's complement includes 9 retrieval specialists.
The vessel's complement includes 1 retrieval supervisor.
The towing capacity of the vessel is 60,000 pounds (27,200 kg).
The recovery ships were delivered in January 1981.
The original owner of the recovery ships was United Technologies Corporation.
MV _Liberty Star_ has been used to tow Space Shuttle external fuel tanks since 1998.
The Space Shuttle external fuel tanks were towed from their assembly plant at Michoud Assembly Facility near New Orleans, Louisiana.
The Space Shuttle external fuel tanks were towed to the Vehicle Assembly Building at the Kennedy Space Center in Florida.
MV _Liberty Star_ served a similar role in recovering the first test flight of the Ares I.
MV _Liberty Star_ was anticipated to continue recovering boosters for the Constellation program.
The Constellation program was canceled in 2010.
MV _Liberty Star_ underwent special strengthening enhancements.
The strengthening enhancements were performed to withstand the greater burden of towing external fuel tanks.
The stern of MV _Liberty Star_ was strengthened at critical points.
New bulwark fairings were added to MV _Liberty Star_.
An H-bitt was installed on MV _Liberty Star_.
Cabling is threaded through the H-bitt to keep it centered during towing operations.
A hydraulic towing winch, also referred to as a double-drum waterfall winch, was installed on MV _Liberty Star_.
The double-drum waterfall winch holds 2,000 feet (610 meters) or more of wire rope on each drum.
One drum of the winch supports booster retrievals.
The other drum of the winch is devoted to external tank towing.
MV _Liberty Star_ has occasionally been used to support scientific research operations.
Scientific research operations included research for the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration.
Scientific research operations included research for several universities.
MV _Liberty Star_ is usually docked alongside its sister ship.
MV _Liberty Star_ is docked at the Solid Rocket Booster processing facility.
The Solid Rocket Booster processing facility is located at the Cape Canaveral Space Force Station in Florida.
Each ship (referring to MV _Liberty Star_ and its sister) is propelled by two main engines.
The main engines provide a total of 2,900 horsepower.
The main engines turn two seven-foot (2.1-meter) propellers.
The propellers have controllable pitch.
Controllable pitch provides greater response time and maneuverability for the ships.
The ships are equipped with two thrusters.
The stern thruster is a water jet system.
The water jet system allows the ship to move in any direction without the use of propellers.
The water jet system was installed to protect the endangered manatee population.
The manatee population inhabits regions of the Banana River where the ships are based.
The water jet system allows divers to work near the ship during operations at a greatly reduced risk.
NASA agreed to transfer the _Liberty Star_ to the U.S. Department of Transportation on August 21, 2012.
The transfer was for the vessel's use as a training vessel at the United States Merchant Marine Academy.
The ship arrived at Kings Point, New York, on September 13, 2012.
Formal turnover of the ship occurred on September 14, 2012.
The vessel was refit for training duty.
The refit included additional berthing.
TV _Kings Pointer_ is the fifth vessel of the Academy to carry that name.
The transfer agreement stipulated that NASA could again use the vessel on future missions if the vessel was available.

